Parliament did not ratify Tymosenko Liberation Amendment
As part of an amendment to a criminal law, the Ukrainian Parliament had rejected the proposed annulment of the paragraph concerning the indictment of Julia Tymosenko, the head of the opposition.
At second reading, MPs had rejected the draft amendment aimed at the reduction of penalties for economic offences, which could have opened the doors of freedom for the former Prime Minister currently in prison.
Tymosenko was sentenced in October to 7 years'imprisonment for reaching an a priori disadvantageous agreement with Russia for the purchase of natural gas.
The judgement is not final, and the court has to rule on the appellant of the convicted person in December.
Tymosenko describes the judgement as political vengeance of the regime and has provoked a process of suspicion of partiality from the tribunal also in the west.
The plan to rescinctate paragraph 365 of criminal law, on the basis of which the former Prime Minister was convicted, was signed by 147 deputies.
It would have taken 226 votes in order to approve it.
