France and the rest of Europe are in the Investor Sight.
A statistic tornado went to Europe on Tuesday.
Each of the states has published the retrieving information to GDP for the 3A.
The Czech economy stagnapped in the last trimester, while France and Germany are growing.
The bond market is breaking all records.
Investors look not only to Italy, but also to Spain, France, Austria and others.
The statistical offices of the euro area, Germany, the Czech Republic and other European countries published earlier their visions of economic development for the third trimester.
The GDP of Germany has a projected growth of 0.5 per cent, that of France by 0.4 per cent.
Both countries changed data for the second trimester when Germany ranked better than expected (growth of .0 per cent instead of 0.0 per cent), however France reported a slight decrease of 0.1 per cent.
The Czech economy grew by 1.5% in the third quarter.
But GDP has stagnated and, according to the Czech Statistical Office, it is confirmed that there is a gradual decline in economic growth.
The results are worse than analysts’ predictions.
The most important events:
The Czech economy grew by 1.5 per cent during the third trimester but stagnapped during the last trimester.
in the third-debt, the German economy grew by 0.5 per cent according to the data forecast and recalculated periodically.
The economy of the euro area and the European Union grew by 0.2% between July and September compared to the previous quarter, in line with what the analysts had anticipated.
The Greek economy fell by 5.2%.
The yield on ten-year bonds is 28.45%
The bond yields of Spain and Italy are climbing to critical limits.
Problems are beginning to be felt in Austria, France, Belgium, etc.
Czech bond yields remain below 4 per cent, while the situation worsens in the region.
There are voices being heard in Germany saying that the ECB should be the guarantee of last instance.
