History of IBM: From slices of Cheese to Flights in the Coss.
International Business Machines (IBM (International Business Machines) was born in New York City a hundred years ago; it was originally known as Computing Tabulating Recording.
It assumed the IBM name in 1924 – initially it used by a branch of a firm in Canada and South America.
The basis of the future IBM was the merger of four firms whose history is related to the year 1980 of the nineteenth century.
They possessed technologies such as the old "pointers", the clocks that recorded the arrival and departure of employees.
The architect of the new company was Charles Ranlett Flint who until 1930 presided over the Supervisory Board.
Flint hired the head of a competing firm Thomas J. Watson to help him run the company.
The leader then shattered the founder of the firm - it was on Watson's ideas that the identity and the culture of the society have so far based.
Watson was the head of the firm in 1952.
IBM, as its name puts it, began with the production of all possible machinery for business and trade – from cheese and meat slicers to cash registers.
In the first four years in which Watson managed, the turnover multiplied to 9 Million Dollars and began the expansion of the firm in all parts of the world.
"Watwas was so irreplaceable that after 40-year leadership it was his son who held the armchair of director, Thomas Watson Junior.
Under his leadership IBM began the development of artificial intelligence and developed the first programming language.
In the '60s IBM people and computers helped NASA to guide flights in the cosmos - on Mercury, Saturn and in 1969 the flight on the Moon.
Meanwhile in 1964, IBM developed the first family of computers – the IBM System/360.
The classic PC that determined the computer standard at the time with the name IBM 5150 came onto the market in 1981.
Twenty years later IBM sold its computer factory to the Chinese company Lenovo, but bought Consulting Pricewaterhouse Coopers and began providing advice and services with high added value.
The IBM successful strategy over the last three years - is the Smarter Planet "a Smarter planet" concept.
Its objective is the clever use of technology in areas not yet exploited, such as transport and city management.
As far as IBM is concerned, the IBM has recently been developing an "Smarter Commerce" platform that helps traders to manage the entire business cycle.
IBM also has a strong history in our territory.
It established its branch in Czechoslovakia in 1932 when it was the first Central and Oriental European country.
Today, in addition to IBM RT, there is also Brno Strategic Outsourcing.
The Central for Central and Oriental Europe sits in Prague and there is also the world-level research and development team dealing with speech-recognition.
