How travel prices have evolved since 1989
Until 1989 there were only 5 state travel agencies in Czechoslovakia: Cedok, Sporturist, CKM, Rekrea and Autotourist.
Travel agencies at that time offered travel mainly in the countries of the socialist bloc, as travel to the west was very limited.
Czech tourists were travelling for holidays in East Germany, the Soviet Union (Sotick, Crimean and the Black Sea), Bulgaria, Romania (both in the Black Sea) and Hungary (Balaton).
A major exception for the period from 1962 to 1972 when it was allowed to travel to Yugoslavia and then the regime began an inclination for the west and travel to Yugoslavia was very limited for Czech tourists.
The great paradox of the socialist era was that it was not permissible for the citizens of the socialist bloc (also for the Czechoslovaks) to travel individually even within the framework of the socialist countries.
In the event that the Czechoslovakia citizen wanted to travel individually, for example in Poland or the Soviet Union, he or she needed a letter of invitation, otherwise the individual journey was not allowed.
Travel agencies at that time also offered trips to the West, it was necessary to queue all over the night with a sleeping bag or a "good acquaintance" within the travel agency.
And even after that it wasn't yet won.
Currency agreement which was the condition for travelling in the west was denied "politically incorrect" people who could not travel at all.
The Czechoslovak currency, however, was not interchangeable and it was not possible to obtain financial resources without the foreign currency agreement (western currency) to travel to the west.
The Czechs most often spent their holidays in the country where there were many country houses and cottages that the Czechs bounted until now.
The travels of the Czechs have experienced a great revolution since 1989 since 1989.
Although until 1989 the vacancies in East Germany and Bulgaria were at most, the Czechs stayed last year abroad 4 million vacation days in countries all over the world.
The great parity of which we are not aware today is that the most popular country is Croatia where it was not possible to move freely until 1989 because part of Yugoslavia at the time.
The abolition of visas in the 1990s of the last century in the western neighbouring countries caused an enormous wave of short-duration visits mainly to Austria to Vienna and Germany.
Czechoslovakia did not have been able to fly to the west for a long time that everyone wanted to see at least for a moment.
e.g. in Paris, travel organizations made Czechs sleep in tents in the parks because our incomes did not allow us to pay for the hotel or a pension.
Typical trait of a trip from Czechs was at that time to take with you his own preserved food, saucooks, etc. to save.
Another important moment was the interchangeability of the Czech koruna, which finally allowed our tourists after decades to freely be able to buy Western Currency.
Our standard of living as well as the average wage have increased, which has caused another important moment around 1997.
Thanks to a growing demand for travel, travel agents have begun since 1997 to prepare towers with air charter links which through contracts have greatly reduced the costs compared to what used to exist.
At the very end of the 1990s, the Czechs had visa-free connections with almost most developed countries around the world apart from the United States, which later facilitated our travel.
Another interesting moment in the history of post-revolutionary tourism, when the first air charter to an exotic country, Thailand, from the Czech Republic.
Since each year and every year direct air charters take Czech tourists to distant hot countries; this winter there will be 12 exotic destinations from the Czech Republic.
More than 100000 Czechs visit exotic countries every year.
Another interesting moment in this period that symbolizes our post-revolutionary development arrived in 2008.
On 17 November 2008, the United States abolished visas for Czech tourists and enabled our country to fully join the group of developed countries.
Also in 2008 Czechs for the first time in history spent more than one-half of their vacations abroad (50.3%) and for the first time in history spent more than 15,000 kroons per person on vacations abroad, including side charges.
The general assembly must find its president, but this is likely to come to an unsolved situation.
Almost another five months Czech Football is without a president and there is a risk that Ivan Hasek's successor at the helm of the Czech Republic Football Association will no longer be known after a General Assembly scheduled to take place in Nymburk on Thursday.
