Agricultural speculation
The German bank examines in a detailed detail the speculation on raw materials
From Christian Teevs
Does the German bank abandon its speculation on raw materials?
According to SPIEGEL-UNLINE, Josef Ackermann commissioned a working group to investigate the consequences of speculation for the world's poor.
In January, the leader of the group then wanted to draw conclusions.
Hamburg -
For the citizens of the Federal Republic, this is clear: according to one study, 84% of Germans find it unacceptable that banks speculate on raw materials such as corn and wheat.
Two-thirds of the respondents even demand that the German bank and other credit institutions discontinue this business avenue because it increases the problems of the world's poorest.
This is the result of a Forsa survey on the order of the consumer organisation Foodwatch.
The study is based on a report presented by Foodwatch in mid-October.
Author Harald Schumann points out in this article that stock exchange speculation on global commodities pushes prices upwards and that the banks share complicity in global starvivation.
The consumer protection campaign aligns concretely against the head of the Deutsche-Bank, Josef Ackermann, even though the big banks such as Goldman Sachs or Morgan Stanley act in the same way.
Specigulation on raw materials was initially introduced to protect merchants from price fluctuations.
But in the meantime, the majority of the experts share the view that gamblers have detached from the bids and demand – and take advantage of the very poor.
Ackermann reacted to critics differently from US competitors: he promised to investigate reprotrust and said, "No business is worth jeopardising Deutsche Bank's good reputation".
According to SPIEGEL ONLINE, an international working group is currently examining Foodwatch-Report charges.
The review has to be completed by the end of the year, the results of the Deutsche Bank's Board of Directors must be presented - usually with recommendation.
At the end of 1 January, the head of the Bank will indicate the consequences to be drawn.
This could lead to a reduction in the market for raw materials, or even a complete drop-down.
Ackermann Buils His Image
Foodwatch manager Thilo Bode welcomed the news, but at the same time confirmed his criticisms of the financial sector: 'Speculation on rising food prices shows in a particularly draconian way that today's banks damage the general interest.
Ackermann is the first banking lobby to be particularly out of obligation today.
"Dirty Bank must remove all investments from its portfolio, for which money has been invested in food speculation," Bode said.
'It doesn't matter to Mr. Ackermann whether it's about starving people or whether Deutsche Bank's reputation is at stake.'
Ackermann’s awareness may seem surprising at first glimpse, but it is clear that the group’s CEO wishes to present a perfect image, a few months before leaving office.
The Deutsche Bank announced on Monday that the Chairman-in-Chief will not make a difference to what is planned in the Management Board.
It could improve its brand image by abandoning controversial agricultural speculation.
With regard to possible losses, he has little to worry - his successor will care.
One in two would terminate the investments on the raw materials.
Foodwatch is trying to maintain great pressure on the Manager.
So far, more than 30,000 netizens have already participated in the organization's campaign.
Bode published the survey on Tuesday, in which on 7 and 8 November The Forsa Institute conducted questionnaires with 1001 people.
Just eleven per cent found it legitimate for Deutsche-Bi Bank to offer investments for which bets on the prices of food products.
Many of the bank's customers would also draw personal consequences if they learned that their bank was involved in such transactions: one out of two would terminate, following the survey, all the investments for which money is injected into commodity speculation.
43 per cent would discourage such a bank from those around them, 4 per cent replied that they would consider terminating their account in order to change their banking institution.
21st House in Vienna
Pavilion that moved
D'Ingeborg Wiensowski
Architecture History with Tops, Botches and a Happy End: The Austrian pavilion for the Brussels World Exhibitions in 1958 won awards, was a museum in Vienna, stayed empty and then fell into disaggregation.
It is now re-opened and serves as an exhibition house.
If we are enthusiastic about modern post-wrangual architecture, for its clear shapes, glass hangars, bright and daring appearances and new building materials which are typical of the belief in technical progress, then the pavilion of Austrian architect Karl schwanzer for the Brussels World Exhibition in 1958 is a good and famous.
Schwanzer Pavilion formerly received the "Great Architecture Pavilion" for its light and suspended steel and glass construction.
Built-in the present-day construction is considered an important step in contemporary Architecture.
He made the architects so famous that he also built the pavilion for the next Austrian Universal Exhibition.
With its legendary four-cylinder BMW house and adjacent bowl-shaped construction in Munich, it eventually became internationally renowned in 1973.
Its pavilion project as a temporary construction had been exhibited after the Universal Exhibition in the Swiss Garden at the South Vienna Square was transformed, and in 1962 a 20th-Century Museum opened -
But the building wasn't physically or technically adapted for a museum -- no walls for hanging works of art, so it wasn't a good environment.
For a long time, however, it was used until contemporary art received a new edifice in Vienna.
Since 2001, the building has been empty and increasingly dilapidated.
Now the architect Adolf Krischanitz has transformed the Pavilion, it is now called "Home of the 21st Century" in the future and will in the future be used as a museum for contemporary art.
On Tuesday evening the Belvédère Museum opened, to which the house belongs, the first exhibition - a stroke of luck from every point of view.
Agnès Husleslein-Arco, who became the new director of the Austrian Gallery Belvédère in 2007.
Husslein-Arco, promoted an art historian to a considerable quarantine, among others in her youth as figure skater, later at Sotheby's, at the Guggenheim Museum and as the founding director of the Salzburg Museum of Modern Art, she carried out particularly the transformational work among politicians and the protection of the monuments, she obtained public and private sponsorship funds.
Excluding contemporaneous art, sculptor Fritz Wotruba whose foundation has been exhibited, a cafe and bookstore, and an outpost must be built.
Strict supervision, small budget
Krischanitz was therefore the ideal performer.
Not only because he had sensivated and sensivated the secession of Joseph Olbracht and had won the competition, but because he had studied at the Technical University of Vienna and for him the pavilion was "extremely an important place" where he saw "practically every weekend" the exhibitions.
He was aware of the original project, and was aware of the problems of the edifice, which lost generosity and leniency as a result of its move to Vienna and its change of use.
During the current transformations, there were great problems: the 'building was 'an energy abyss,' says Krischanitz.
Window profiles of glass facades were changed, insulator fiberglass was raised and the roof replaced by a particular safety glass.
Heactic deck girders have been reared or replaced, and asbestos-filled linseions have been rehabilitated.
Two subsilations made room for the new operation, a dim-down panel of light between the house and the road provides lighting, a deck driven to the entrance - this is almost a new construction, but still the light Schwanzer Pavilion.
Original exterior gates remained, the house was painted in a shade protection against rust of red-brown color.
And the movie theater is even exactly the same what it used to be.
And all of this under close supervision by the authorities that are responsible for monuments and with a small budget.
'Often good and unpopular materials had to be found, and which also please the Monuual Protection Office,' says Krischanitz and he draws attention to raw soil in the basement.
Despite all the constraints, problems and changes, the edifice remained flexible and light.
It would have been difficult with the Pavilion, "which came from Brussels," Krischanitz said recently, a typical "Drop-in-the-City story."
Such an Architecture lies "always in the wrong place" and still wants to "awake the good conscience".
But in this case the House of the 21st century is not in the wrong place, because if it is a stroke of luctation of urban construction: around the Jardin Suisse stands the newly planned "central station district", with offices and apartments.
And another stroke of luck may be going to happen: the 21st-century House could benefit from the reinforcement of another Krischanitz building, because Francesca von Habsburg collector has bought the Berlin temporary art centre, and as it is said, it is already negotiating with the city in order to obtain a site in the vicinity of the Swiss Garden.
