For centuries, Swiss bankers have resided here: from the national bank via the Palais du Crédit Suisse to elitist private banks such as Julius.
We arrive in the entrance hall of the UBS through a heavy gate.
Inside, the walls are coated with marble, in front are thick leather loungers, and next to the logo of the bank, a large label for "safe".
UBS knows what the customer is looking for here: security.
This idea of the Swiss bank has at some point removed from the market – or betraying the two major Swiss banks, UBS and Credit Suisse, as the head of a small Swiss private bank finds it.
He would prefer not to read its name in the newspaper, ultimately like all Swiss bankers it does business also with the two big banks.
UBS would have become far too big, he finds - especially in bank investment, where the Anglo-Saxons would also have spread.
There would be nothing left for the Swiss to say.
"Although they have long been adapted to Anglo-Saxon morals."
This was the Anglo-Saxon investment bank that changed UBS, made high profits and then the debacle: no European financial institution lost so much money in the sub-prime jungle like the big Swiss bank.
The disaster became apparent on October 15, 2008.
'On Wednesday the government coalition of the small country of the seven-million-strong population undertook the largest rescue operation in the history of their nation,' writes author Lukas Hossig in his book 'The Crash of the UBS.
With up to 68 billion CFA francs, i.e. 45 billion euros, the politicians agreed to save the big bank from bankruptcy.
How could this happen?
If we talk with Swiss bankers, they see the problem especially at the height of UBS.
In June 1998, two of the three major Swiss banks jointly concluded a huge group.
A bank had been set up, which was already too large for little-Swish, and had a constant increase.
What has helped is that UBS’s two pre-receipient banks have completed: a wealthy, lethargical, asset-management-intensive bank in zurich and a poorer one, but certainly already more heavily internationalised.
Suddenly, the international investment banks had favourable access to a lot of money, with which they were able to shoot an enormous wheel.
Two names are associated with UBS ascension: Peter Wuffli from 2001 to 2007 Head of the UBS Group, and Marcel Ospel, former Chairman of the Board.
'I've always thought that Wuffli and Ospel were a dream team,' said bank researcher Hans Geiger.
'Wuffli, the great humble intellectual, who gets along very well into models and stats, and Ospel, the intuitive, who made its way to the top with learning the bank."
But Ospel would have trusted Wuffli until the end.
And wuffli would probably have made a decisive error at the end: "He thought his models were the reality," says Geiger.
"It's a tragic fall."
Tragic, maybe.
One thing is clear: the exuberance happened before the fall.
UBS staff clearly rescind the leadership meetings at which Wuffli Group leader always gave the same slogan: dethrone Goldman Sachs.
The U.S. bank was the most powerful investment bank in the world.
She commanded "League Tables" the rankings, which at that time documented the success of the banks.
After all, UBS wanted to go upstairs.
Certain steps have already been successful.
It played a very important role in foreign currencies and securities transactions.
Corporate advisors, who were responsible for this, however, pointed out a weakness: in international interest transactions, in Fixed Income banking jargon there would be a huge difference compared to the largest banks.
UBS would have gaps in US Mortgage Products.
To fill this lacupgrature, the UBS embarked on an adventure, which almost cost it its lives.
Thirified for growth, it is both the bank itself and a management fund created before it in US sub-prime investments that have invested.
It evolved into a massive machine that packaged and passed value documents based on American mortgages – but also kept risks.
Even when other banks had long been trying to go down.
'Still currently in the summer of 2007, while the American real estate market was on fire, the UBS bought there,' says Geiger, the banking expert.
The greater the euphoria, the sharper the fall.
One manager afterwards had to deceive, when putting an end to the attempts turned out was more difficult than previously thought.
UBS posts have become ejectivable and have so far been pilgish.
Whoever runs the big bank has an almost unimprovable task.
It must find a strategy for a bank, for which all the suggested business models have disappeared.
Bank researcher Geiger already has an idea: to put an end to the investment bank, a concentration in asset management and a return to Swiss roots.
But the bank don't (yet) want to go that far.
But the investment bank must atrophy vigorously, to the extent that it is clear so far.
Next Thursday bank manager Sergio Ermotti wants to present his future strategy to the world.
The 51-year-old Italo-Swishi was only assumed to be on a transitional basis when the bank dissociously dissociated from its predecessor Grabel.
In the meantime, it has become abreast: it has to carry out UBS business in a sustainable way, the Bank of zurich said on Tuesday.
Ermotti wants to return the investment bank to progress in the mid-1990s.
It cancels 3,500 jobs.
But it won't be enough.
THE UBS needs a vision.
Who's it?
What does she want to make money with?
These are the questions Axel Weber has certainly already been asked.
So far, he only says publicly that he was going to fetch with his wife an apartment in zurich zurich.
Next year.
