Sequelation of personal mass
"The Beauty and the Sorrow: An Intimate History of the First World War", it detonates with a long list of character clenching your heart.
One piece of tips to start with: tear up this page carefully and make it your bookmark.
You'll get to know these people in great detail. In this romantic narrative of Peter Englund, this list of characters will be your GPS, a beacon during these few moments when, like one of its men or women, you'll be troubled and desperate in the mists of the war.
Peter Englund is a Swedish historian and journalist.
He is also the new permanent secretary of the Swedish Academy, awarded the Nobel Prize for Literature.
Here he wrote an extraordinary book that he accurately described as "an anti-historic work."
It contains a few well-equenged names, major treatises or famous battles; there are almost no ambassadors or discussions on strategies and material.
It'sn't so much a book about what's happened," he explains, "a book describing what it used to look like." It includes "feelings, impressions, experiences and humours."
"Beautifulness and sorrow" summed up the wartime experiences of more or less ordinary men and women, on both sides of the war, from young schoolgirls and botanists to scalators, doctors, ambulance drivers and vendors.
Some will become heroes.
Others prisoners of the war; some will lose limbs, go mad or lose their lives.
