Scholarship union sued the city for redundancy
The trade union, which accounted for hundreds of school aids who had lost their jobs last month, plans to sue the city on Wednesday for unnecessary and discriminatory lay-offs, with a disproportionate impact on schools accommodating disadvantaged schoolchildren.
Prosecution is probably the ultimate weapon of the 37th District Council in an attempt to rescind the layoffs.
Sixty-seven school assistants, parental co-ordinators and other employees, among the lowest-paid in the city, lost their jobs on 7 October; trade union leaders compared these layoffs to political revenge for not letting the city gain access to a health-care fund led by groups of workers, which would have made it possible to close the budget gap last spring.
The municipal authorities categorically denied this accusation.
At a city council hearing last month, Chancellor Dennis M. Walcott stated that larunties constituted part of the budget closed in June.
A member of the Council retaliated, mentioning that the dismissals of school assistants were in no way specified in the budget.
Council officers of the 37th District have stated that they will complete a notice of claim, informing the Department of Education that it would be prosecuted; a press conference will be organized on Wednesday to formally announce the legal proceedings at the Manhattan State Supreme Court.
Dennis Mr. Walcott and the Department of Education of the city will constitute the respondent; the plaintiffs will be represented by eight dismissed female workers, all of black or of Latin-American origin, like most of the school assistants who have lost their jobs.
Elizabeth Thomas, a spokeswoman for the city's legal department, stated that the municipality had not yet been able to see the reasons for the trial and therefore could not make any comments on the allegations.
According to an outline of complaints obtained by The New York Times, the union would rely on the number of layoffs in schools in the most disadvantaged areas of the city to support its complaint against unfair treatment, stating that the poorest schools would lose the most aid as a result of regular underfunding.
While there was no dismissal on Staten Island, and just a few at Upper East Side or TriBeCa, 17 school assistants lost their jobs in the 23rd District of Brownsville, and 46 in the 8 and 9th Southern Bronx Districts.
Noting the loss of five out of every eight school assistants in Harlem Public School, where 68 per cent of schoolchildren live in poverty, the lawsuits state that "It is not an affluent parent yard that can bring money to fill the budget gap."
The budget for public school 36 has been reduced by 3.26% this year; funds have also been lost when the city has changed its calculation of the additional funding for each underprivileged student enrolment.
The trade union also states that high school principals may have been misleaded by certain terms in a memo authorizing the dismissal of parental coordinators.
The memo used the term "exceivability" to describe the action, even if "exceivability" more generally describes the action of displacing a teacher from a school to a group of available workers whose salaries are paid by the central office.
