Language may be harmless, but the debate on Libya has never been simple until now.
In this summer, legislators from both parties came into conflict, as well as with the White House, on the formulation of several resolutions aimed at whether or not the United States military role in the NATO mission in Libya.
In June, Kerry and McCain presented a resolution to authorize the limited use of US military forces in Libya.
The draft law had been defeated by a broad bipartisan margin and had never been voted on in the Senate.
But on the same day when the bill failed, the Chamber of Deputies also rejected a measure aimed at reducing the financing of US operations in Libya.
The U.S. mission stayed in the form of legislative purgatory – conunicipated enough to criticize Obama’s handling of the mission, but not enough to cut the grass under its feet.
The indecisiveness of contemporaneousness was overtaken by events.
Tripoli fell under insurgents in August and Gaddafi was killed on 20 October.
The NATO mission officially concluded on 31 October.
The Senate can now take action.
The Foreign Relations Committee resolution "congratulates the Libyan people for their extraordinary courage and determination with a view to freedom" and "congratilises the men and women of the American armed forces and their coalition partners engaged in military operations to Protect the people of the people of the people of Libya for their fantasy courage and professionalism".
The resolution also states 'the national interest of the United States in achieving an irreversible and successful transition to democracy in Libya.
Will the new measure gain all the support of the Senate Foreign Affairs Committee or will divide thrillers as last June?
Senator and Commission Chairman Richard D. Lugar (Independent) opposed the latest version.
Its spokeswoman stated last Monday that Richard D. Lugar had not yet read the most recent version of a new project.
Within the Capitol, the House has to vote this week for an amendment to the Constitution to obtain a balanced budget, a legislative priority for almost all congressional Republicans, and some Democrats.
The measure, supported by the Republican Bob Goodlatte (Virginia), would constitute the first constitutional amendment on the budget, or any other matter, to obtain a floor vote in the House or in the Senate.
Other genuilities have so far failed to get their amendments approved, but it is not without trying.
Goodlatte’s amendment is one of the 68 constitutional amendments to be tabled in the Congress on a wide range of topics.
Some are recurrent - the same bill proposed both in the House and in the Senate - and others vary slightly from a precedent.
There are at least 15 versions in the House solely of the amendment for a balanced budget, and another handful that "would control federal spending".
Some of these bills are the same, but give their supporters - in particular the new Republicans who wish to gill their CV - the opportunity to boast of being "the author" of such a measure.
The Budget Control Act approved last August called for the vote of the House and the Senate on the amendment of a balanced budget.
In March, 58 senators voted in favour of a non-constituent resolution supporting such an amendment.
Beyond the budget, several members wish to limit the number of terms during which congressional members can serve, while José E. Serrano (Democrate - New York) wishes to lift the limit of two terms for presidents.
Jesse L. Jackson (Democrate - Illinois) proposed a plethorous amendments guaranteeing the right to quality education and health-care, among other topics.
An amendment forbidding same-sex marriage is perpetually proposed, as is the amendment prohibiting the desecration of the flag.
Some legislators also wish to authorize the annulment of federal laws if two-thousand states are opposed.
As greedy for members to offer amendments, they are not as fast as in the past.
At the 111th Congress, legislators proposed 77 amendments, introduced 66 at the 110th.
The pace is slowing down compared to the early 1990s, when members generally proposed more than 150 amendments every two years, according to figures provided by the Senate library.
But these amendments were not much more successful than they do today.
The last amendment to be added - the 27th - was ratified in 1992 but proposed to Congress in 1789.
