Bell walls have eyes: researchers study you on Facebook
Prior to becoming the new face of the extreme right in Europe, Anders Behring Breivik was a simple guy expressing his anti-immigrant opinions online.
On Monday, Breivik, who admitted to having been taken from murderous magnificence in Norway in the month of July leaving 77 victims, was confronted with his first trial.
While Breivik acted lonely, he walked away from being on cyberspace: he had spent a large part of the time preceding the attack on his computer, talking with some of the Millions of Nationalists supporting far-right groups on social networking sites.
After the tragedy of this summer, researchers want to learn more about these people.
But how do we Find them?
Easy... log in to Facebook.
'We have realized that it isn't so difficult to find them again,' said Jamie Bartlett, lead author of a report recently published by DEMOS think tank on digital populism in Europe.
“Facebook is replete with so much personal information, Jamie Bartlett says, that researchers could simply use the site’s advertising tool to find the people they’re looking for with scientific accuracy – the way merctacigators have been doing it for years.
Jamie Bartlett team found that half a million members are fans of extreme right-wing groups in Europe and targeted them with advertisements, but instead of linking them to a new group or product, the advertisements invited users to respond to a survey. The questions raised concerned their level of education, their attitudes to the violence and their optimism about their own future.
By coming into contact with these people through Facebook, Jamie Bartlett and his colleagues were able to question 10,000 supporters of 14 far-right parties in 11 European countries – without leaving the office.
'This is a new way of doing research,' says Jamie Bartlett.
Some of the results are not at all astonishing: online supporters of right-wing groups are men, both young and anti-immigrant.
Surprinably, "those that combine online and offline activism are more reasonable, more democratic, and less violent than those who stay behind their computer screen."
DEMOS is just an example of how Facebook has become an inescapable new tool in the hands of scientists.
Reflection groups, medical researchers and political scientists use the site to study all topics related to health issues such as social trends, thanks to "Like", publications on the walls and up-to-date statutes.
With more than 800 Millive Active Users adding an average of three content per day, "supernova of data" generates an explosion in research, increasing the number of research works whose site name appears in the title by almost 800% compared to the last five years.
For some scholars, the advantage of Facebook is that it allows you to study people that you couldn't approach on the street.
'Beforeshoe when you tried to investigate people in the British National Party, it was very difficult to identify them.'
"You had to go to the party, but they wouldn't have allowed you to come in," said Jamie Bartlett.
"Facebook avoids all of this - you have direct access to their information."
Others stated that the site could offer a means of identifying and addressing health and social problems.
According to a recent study by D. Megan Moreno from the University of Wisconsin-Madison, and his colleagues, young students who talked on Facebook about their drunken exploits were extremely likely to be alcoholics than discreet students about this.
Megan Moreno suggests that student colleagues, such as councillors, could monitor the site and intervene to possibly assist a student whose status would talk too often about alcohol.
"You can't handle a problem if you don't diagnose it", Dimitri Christakis, co-author of Megan Moreno and director of Child Health, Behaviour and Development Center at the Seattle Children's Research Institute, says the Washington Post.
'We know how to identify at-risk children, who would in any other case not be recognized.'
Facebook also constantly accesses the information of its users.
The data-managing site team studied statistics on the loving situation and Valentine's Day, the turn-operating rate in the 2010 mid-mandate elections and an index of national Happiness (thanks to available information only – the champions of digital freedom have long argued that the site could make its treasury of data available to others for research purposes).
But Facebook users know that the site observes them, whether they like it or not - in exchange for being able to play Scrabble with a friend who is thousands of kilometers away, the site is allowed to access your information.
But what happens when Facebook users are part of an investigation they haven't even heard of?
While researchers have well-determined instructions on how to collect information other than via the Internet, there is something else online.
"I don't think a large number of users have already thought about the fact that a researcher can study their profile," says Neil Selwyn, a sociologist at the London Knowledge Laboratory, according to a 2006 study by looking at the Facebook walls of his students.
"As far as they know, the exchanges were seen only by their friends."
Then there comes the question of methodology.
Even without going through the Internet, there is no guarantee that the topic of a study is completely honest.
On Facebook, it is impossible to know the degree of truth of a profile or on the wall of a user.
'What you're saying on Facebook and what you're doing in reality are two totally different things,' says Neil Selwyn.
That is why, despite his experience as a tech researcher, Neil Selwyn believes that a notebook and a pen remain the best tools you can use.
'Nothing can replace the real world and in contact with people,' he said.
"Social studies are supposed to address the social aspect - and the social isn't in front of a computer."
