7 Le Lessons Learned Steve Jobs' Lost Interview in Cinema This Week
After Steve Jobs died on October 5, some compared it to Henry Ford – distinguished by technical genius and inventor of modern computer science.
But what Apple fans can find more revealing in an interview with Steve Jobs recently rediscovered, which will be released this week in the first part of a film in the main markets, is the extent to which this creative spirit understood the processes and workflows of products.
Undoubtedly a creator and a dreamer. He identified with a hippie but was also very meticulous and organized, as well as an organizational enhancer - a man who believed that many business executives were suffering "disease letting them to think that a brilliant idea makes 90 percent of the job... [but a considerable amount of manual labor separates a great idea from a product."
In 1995, Bob Cringely developed the television series "Triumph of the Nerds." This series retraculates the origin of the computer, and it sits for more than an hour with Steve Jobs for a long and rare conversation.
At that time, Steve Jobs had to leave Apple for about 10 years. He was already working hard on his new computer company NeXT, aiming for popular adoption of the Internet.
While a small part of Cringely was used in Nerds, he stated that the main copies disappeared in transportation.
It was only after Steve Jobs's death that a complete video was found in the director's garage.
This slightly re-mounted video is the basis of the 68-minute film Steve Jobs: The Lost Interview.
For someone known to hating in-depth interviews, Steve Jobs seems surprisingly excited to outline technological philosophies and his business strategies.
And unbelievably careful.
Out of four different points during the interview, the answers come a long time after the questions – between 10 and 15 seconds of reflection, during which Steve Jobs clearly weighs his thoughts, in order to provide an accurate answer.
We also discover moments when Steve Jobs' perfectionism resurrects, when he seems eager to hear Cringely's questions, beating him elbow to speed it up and move to questions or topics of greater importance.
circuited before Pixar, iPod or the iPhone, we find something strangely prescicient on Lost Interview. Steve Jobs assesses what he sees good or bad among other companies and shares his own vision on the future of computing.
It's one thing to read Steve Jobs' career posthumous assessments, but there's something raw and inspiring about the man we're seeing here, the anxious dreamer on the eve of grandeur.
It has a vision of how things can and should be.
He explains them and then the dishes as a follow-hut-went-off application.
Here is a quick rehearsal of seven remarkable sequences from Lost Interview:
On the way he has learned to lead a company:
"Throughout these years, I've always said, 'Why are you doing things?" and the answers are constantly the same: 'oh, just because you have to do them.'
No one knows why, no one thinks deeply about things in a company.
That's what I discovered.
Steve Jobs goes on to give details of his efforts to simplify Apple accounting.
Perplex as to the way in which the costs were recorded - we often started with a "standard cost" that is valued on a nose-to-obvious basis and then adjusted with a "variation" - it developed an automated plant that ensured the precise calculation of commercial costs.
The Pope is an advisability to play a trick:
Steve Jobs recounts the anecdote of the "Blue Box" he created with Steve Wozniak – a device used to hack into telephone networks and make a long-distance phone.
The device has been the subject of much writing beforehand, but Steve Jobs' euphoria here in his description of the Blue Box as an act of appropriation is contagious.
It describes the manner in which he and Wozniak tested the box by using a public phone, by making a call and then by connecting from an AT-T network to another while by bypassing as many satellites as possible.
"We wrapped the bucket of different things half a dozen times and screamed in the public phone and it would happen to you a minute later to the local public phone," Jobs laughed.
"We were young and thanks to this Blue Box, we learned that we ourselves could create something capable of controlling billions of dollars of infrastructure all over the world."
We didn't both know very much, but we didn't know a little thing that could control great things. It was an incredible lesson, and I don't think Apple would ever have existed without it.
Steve Jobs goes on to talk about a marvellous lathe that he and Wozniak developed: he telephoned the Vatican with the Blue Box in the middle of the Night passing for Henry Kissinger and asking to talk to the Pope.
While several members of the Catholic hierarchies were summoned in the middle of the night to talk to the American diplomat, the two accomplices laughed just before the Pope himself was woken up.
About his love with technology (and Bill Hewlett's canvassing):
At the age of 12, chassing spare parts to make a frequency meter, he searched for Bill Hewlett number in the directory and gave him a phone call.
Soon after their 20-minute conversation, Steve Jobs gets a part-time Summer job at Hewlett-Packard.
"That had a remarkable influence on me. It was the only company that I'd seen at that age and it formed my definition of a company and the way in which employees are treated."
Steve Jobs later became one of the HP employees visiting the company's research labs, located in Palo Alto, where he unveiled the "first desk desk computer ever designed."
It was as tall as a pellet, had a little cathode-ray tube. I fell in love with it.
The teenager that I had went back to HP and started typing programs for this machine.
On the improvisation of innovation:
At several times in the interview, Steve Jobs talks about inventing new products at a whim.
At the beginning of his quarantine, while installed to sell a few printed circuit boards, a customer asked him to assemble a complete computer.
Working at only 30 days of credit, he had to deal with the assembly as well as the delivery of the finished appliances.
Later at Apple, he remembered the difficulties in developing a computer mouse:
"I'm rescinding, I was screaming at, prescribing that it would take me five years [to make a mouse that would cost . .
Eighty-ninety days later, we had a mouse that we could make for 15 dollars that was incredibly reliably reliable.
On large companies that are running out of steam:
Long before it revived Apple, Steve Jobs had anticipated how many industrial executives were going to mislead and lose control of their market share.
"Let's say you were working at IBM or Xerox, did you make a better copier or a better printer?"
You have a monopoly on the market, so the company doesn't have any more success.
Selling and marketing can make it evolve, so [it's are these types of people) who end up directing companies and product personnel are sidelined from decision-making.
The genius in production that led to this monopoly position is completely thrifting by people who cannot conceive a good product in relation to a bad product - the manual labor required.
This is what happened in Xerox...
Xerox could have owned the entire computing industry.
Xerox would have been the Microsoft in the 1990s...
They wrested the defeat from victory.
On innovation as an artistic form:
'A huge piece of manual work separates an awesome idea from an exceptional product... as you develop this idea, it changes and grows.'
You learn a lot about its subtleties.
There are trade-comparables to do... Some things you can't do, electrons can, glass, robots, factories too.
You have to keep thousands of things in mind (those concepts) by assembling them to each other.
First of all, decision-making is made out of personal taste. It's about trying to exposing yourself to the best products that men have created, and trying to bring these things to the product that you're creating.
Picasso once said that good artists copy and that great artists fly.
We are always ashamed of stealing excellent ideas.
This is in part what makes Macintosh exceptional. The people who worked on it were musicians, poets, zoologists, historians, who simply proved to be the best computer scientists in the world.
If it didn't for computer science, they would have done extraordinary things in a different field.
Scalia and Thomas dine with opponents of health law as the court reunites
On the day the Supreme Court meets behind closed doors to address the issue of political discord, namely whether the opposition to President Obama's health law will be heard, two of its judges, Antonin Scalia and Clarence Thomas, were noticed at a dinner sponsored by the law firm that will defend the case in the High Court.
The event took place last Thursday, when the nine judges gathered for a conference to discuss petitions for consideration.
One of the cases in question was a lawsuit filed by 26 states challenging the ambitious health-care reform voted by Congress last year, a law that was a rallying point for conservative activists at the national level.
The judges have agreed to listen to the complaint, in fact, a monumental argument of 5.30 a.m. is expected next March, and the result is likely to help destabilize the 2012 presidential race, which will burgeoning at the moment when the court's decision is handed down.
The lawyer in front of the court and will argue about the rejection of the law is likely to be Paul Clement, who served as United States Solicitor General during the administration of George W. Bush.
Clément’s law firm PLLC Bancroft was one of the two dozen law firms that helped sponsor the Federalist Society, a long-standing group dedicated to defending conservative legal principles.
Another law firm that sponsored the Dinner, Jones Day, represents one of the trade associations that challenged the law, the National National Federation of Independent Business.
Another promoter was the pharmaceutical giant Pfizer Inc, which presents a huge amount of financial stake in the outcome of the dispute.
Dinner was held at a D.W.T. hotel a few hours a few hours after the court conference on the case.
Among other things, Mitch McConnell, the top Republican of the Senate and declared adversary of the Healthy Act.
The guests featured at Dinner? Scalia and Thomas.
This is not new: the two judges were present at the events of the Fédéralist Society for years.
And there is nothing that runs counter to the rules of ethics.
In fact, judges are exempted from a code of conduct that governs the actions of federal judges.
If they were, they would no doubt argue through Canon Code 4C, which states: “A judge may attend fund-his collections of organizations relating to the law and others even though the judge may not be an intervener, guest of honour, or on the programme of such an Event”.
However, the mere presence of Scalia and Thomas in the vicinity of two law firms in the case, as well as a company with a massive financial interest, was enough to alarm government ethics activists.
"This violation of ethics and superb indifference to the code contradicts the statements of several judges that the Court respects the same rules applicable to all other federal judges," said Bob Edgar, President of Common Cause.
“Judges drowned and dredging and raising gala funds with lawyers with cases pending before the court”.
Their presence and participation in fund-raising of this event demonstrates any claim of impartiality, and is unacceptable.
Scalia and Thomas showed little regard for critics asserting that they all too easily mix court cases with the agenda of groups such as the Fédéralist Society.
And Thomas's wife, Ginni, is a high-profile conservative activist.
On the other hand, the curators claim that Judge Elena Kagan has an ethical problem, not Scalia and Thomas.
Kagan was Solicitor-General of the Obama Administration when the first objections to the law were brought to the court of first instance.
Her critics lobbied that Kagan recused himself from hearing the case, saying that she had invested too much in defending the law to be unbiased.
Kagan gave no indication that she would do so.
“Boei has a record USD 18-bill” order for airliners
