Cliquary adversity has held back the economy
In the first year of management of Mario Lopez Valdez, in Sinaloa unemployment and informality have increased, foreign investment has fallen and the economy contracted
2011 will be remembered in Sinaloa's economic history as the first year of the Government of Mario Lopez Valdez, the self-request for change, which raised great expectations with regard to the advances of the economy, but given climate adversity at the beginning of the year, the increase does not be observed, in exchange there is a deterioration in the main indicators of economic development.
The low temperatures of February, apart from causing losses of trillions for the agricultural sector, limited the possibilities of the State economy for growth, caused a contraction in economic activity in general by 3.6 per cent in the first half of the year, justified mainly by the historical decline of 31.16 per cent in agriculture, which affected the bubbing of other economic sectors.
According to Frozen Assignments by the Mexican Council for Sustainable Rural Development, there was damage of 582.835 hectares cultivated, of which 78.03 per cent suffered total damage and the remaining 21.07 per cent suffered partial damage, the main crops affected were vegetables, corn and chickpeas.
The fall in economic activity generated, until November, a steady increase in unemployment in the State, according to the National Institute of Statistics and Geography the state unemployment rate during the first three trimester of the first three trimester of the year there was an accelerated increase, because while from January to March, 55,053 sinaloens were unreater, with a rate of 4.53 per cent of Active Economic Population.
Less liquidity
Unemployment in Sinaloa during the year is multi-factor, according to the economist Gerardo Lopez Cervantes, director of the UAS Faculty of Economic and Social Sciences, the increase in unemployment can be explained indirectly depending on the actions carried out by public policy.
'What we can see is an increase in unemployment which is due to a number of factors, in which the loss of liquidity in the market due to a restriction of resources by legislation approved in congressional to combat money-laundering, because for people who earn dollars in an illicit way it is not easy for them to work to earn them, invest them and use them in something,' he says.
Miscellaneous detention
Lepez Cervantes pointed out that the increase in unemployment is also due to a demographic factor.
'The number of people abandoning the state to work abroad in the United States has been limited, first because of entry difficulties, and secondly because by entering they already have a lot of difficulty in finding a job, these people are staying here and asking for sources of work, which means that unemployment is increasing,' he said.
Lack of seriousness
The analysis of the salian labour market is worsening by considering the high level of lack of seriousness that predominates between working conditions, according to INEGI, 60.38 per cent of the population employed in INEGI works without co-contributing as a worker with any social insurance institution.
In the absence of unemployment and a lack of seriousness, there is another reason for demarcation for the state, the entity has the lowest wages in the country, according to the records of the Mexican Social Insurmandy Institute and the Labour and Social Foresight Secretariat.
The average monthly income of Sinaloian workers was 5,000,235 pesos, well below the national average of 7,375 pesos until September.
Trend of FDI
Apart from the decrease in economic activity and the increase in unemployment, in 2011 there was the largest reduction in foreign direct investment over the last 10 years, according to the Economic Affairs Secretariat from January to June, State FDI reached USD 630 1,697, a decrease of USD 87.74 compared to USD 5,143,312 in the same period of the previous year.
In turn, the Economic Development Secretariat of the State Government accounts for a total investment of 17,280 trillion pesos per year, recorded mainly by companies in the tertiary sector.
Deficiency
Given the scenario of economic contraction, in July, the Government of the United States applied to the public congress for the approval of a credit of 3 trillion pesos for investment in infrastructure projects in the 18 municipalities, with the goal of reactivating the economy, the approval of the State congress was 2.6 trillion pesos, and until November there was no idle profit in any sector.
Reactions
According to the representatives of the employer's sector, Mario Lopez Valdés's first-year of Government management demonstrates a lack of managerial capacity of the Government.
The President of the National Transformational Industry Chamber Miguel Loredo Lopez stated that in 2011 there was no harmonisation of the wishes of the various sectors, including the Government of the State.
'The efficiency of Sinaloa's economy during this year has been really very poor, we have to work intensively to recover the ground we lost this year, in 2012 we have to double our efforts, we have had to align everyone's effort and, above all, the already synchronized good progress of this new government team, let's hope that next year is a lot better,' he said.
Economic smoothing
Loredo Lopez stated that events such as frosts at the beginning of the year retained the state economy and restricted its yield and growth.
'Little economic flux is produced from several events, between which jelly frosts, one of the main effects has been a slow economy which does not rotate fast enough to generate economic dynamism that makes it possible to have an acceptable level in litigation, products and services and therefore, at the end of the year, we close much below the expectations that we had,' he says.
Redefining vocation
Teodoro Salazar Uriart, President of the Patron Confederal Confederation of the Mexican Republic of Sinaloa, stated that we want to channel the State towards more dynamic productive activities.
'In the general terms, we have observed that we have worked, we believe that it is necessary to develop a long-term programme which redefines the vocation and economic direction of the State, because it is important to detonate the full potential of Sinaloa's material and human resources,' he says.
A lot to do
The Coparmex President stressed the importance of establishing a vision of the future that better channels the Government's efforts.
'In general terms, I would say that the Government has made efforts to yield good results in different areas, in terms of economic development there are a few advances, but it is important to develop a long-term programming,' he said.
He pointed out that the current administration had shown advances in the management of public policy.
“There are elements that allow us to say that the Government is more inclusive, what I can say is a great deal of work, and the Government is aware that there is still a great deal to be done,” he says.
