The new Italian government will be formed on Wednesday
Prime-Deposited Prime Minister Mario Monti will meet with the President of Italy Tomorrow to present the new government that will deal with a crisis on the verge of economic disaster
Prime Minister-designate Mario Monti will meet with the President of Italy on Wednesday to present the new government that will deal with a crisis that brought Italy to the brink of an economic disaster and jeopardized the entire Eurozone.
A presidential palace communiqué announced that Monti, appointed on Sunday, would meet with President Giorgio Napolitano on Wednesday to confirm that he could train the government.
It is awaited to present a cabinet composed in its majority by technocrats, even if it is not clear when the new executive assumes its job.
Monti has said that he will present to the president the results of his conversations with the political parties in his attempt to form a government.
"I want to confirm my absolute confidence in the ability of our country to overcome this difficult phase", said the former European Commissioner for Journalists.
Monti did not state explicitly that he could form a government, but the tone of his comments indicated that the obstacles had been overcome.
'Framework is now well charted,' he added.
Monti completed the process of forming a government in less than three days, in a shorter time than usual, while Italy was fighting a political and financial crisis that pushed the costs of its debt to unsubscribable levels.
The new administration led by former European Commissioner Monti must approve a tough austerity bundle required by the European leaders to regain confidence in Italy that was affected.
By pointing the pressure on Monti to hurry, the instability of the markets has placed the yield of Italy's 10-year-old BTP bonds above 7 per cent, the level at which Greece and Ireland have been rescued.
Emma Marcegaglia, leader of the Italian boss Confindustria, told the media after her meeting with Monti: 'We have said that we will support this government a great deal.
We believe that this Government is the last opportunity for Italy to emerge from this emergency.
Monti’s chances of success were spurred considerably by the support of Silvio Berlusconi’s PDL party, to which the crisis forced to resign last Saturday.
'We believe that the efforts of Professor Monti will have a good result,' Angelino Alfano, secretary of the centre-to-right party, told the press.
The support of PDL, the tallest Italian party, is significant because until now many of its members had been opposed to the technocratic-majority-majority government that Monti forms.
Parliamentary support
Monti’s new government must have strong parliamentary support to implement what impopoly unpopular austerity rescits are likely.
Any blockage or lagging in its efforts could provoke a new and devastating attack on the financial markets.
The Italian Association of Foreign Benches has added to the pressure, observing that a bankruptcy of Monti would be a disaster.
Monti began its consultations on Monday with political parties, trade unions and employer groups and youth and women's organisations.
He will finish his meeting tour on Tuesday at Night.
Monti was appointed on Sunday by Napolitano, which empowered an extremely rapid transition in response to the crisis.
After a brief respite at the end of last week, when it was made clear that Berlusconi would resign, the costs of Italian debt have now returned to critical levels between uncertainty about the success of the new prime minister.
Sweeping Italy, with public debt of EUR 1.9 trillion, this would be too much for the current financial defence of Eurozone.
Monti had said that his Government should continue until the future elections, scheduled for 2013, despite the widespread predictions that politicians would only give it time to implement the reforms before advancing the elections.
Monti had said that he would like to include politicians in his cabinet, but the major parties insisted that it should only be formed by specialists, an indication of their objections to a process forced by financial pressure.
Political sources pointed out that mutual distrusts and disagreements between parties complicated the attempt to include political figures.
