"Drunks are good, but Valle didn't do them.
Its body has never been its asset and it has lost because of this aspect."
For this prize, which the National Beauty Contest presented for for the first time, after the parade in a bathing jersey arose a cruel prediction that took place last night, it was said that it was a small gift that Raymundo made to Melina so as not to give it the taste of the wreath of the wreath.
Reality or not, the truth is that Melina today begins a year of reign, not as a sovereign, but as a princess, a position that gives it the opportunity to represent Colombia in an international competition by designating the National Contest.
However, it will pass in history as one of the queens who, despite its dazzling pass through Cartagena, did not obtain the crown.
Thus was the work of Cali who identified the corpse of Alfonso Cano
A dactyloscopist of Cali has moved on to history as the man who confirmed "Alfonso Cano".
The work of these civil servants is crucial.
In the past on 4 November, a typists from the CTI of Cali went into history as the person who had confirmed the identity of Farc's supreme leader: "Alfonso Cano", by comparing his fingerprints.
Of the seven typists working in the CTI's local offices this week, this week it was the "availability" tour of Eliazar Gonzalez, a man from Puerto Tejada who has been working for 21 years in the field of judicial investigation.
At the beginning of his career, Gonzalez had worked in the municipalities, where many times he had to spend more than five days in the forest to arrive where the corpses that needed to be identified.
In the year 2000 it was transferred to the capital of Valle and, after taking appropriate courses, it specialized in typing, which is the study of friction skin, i.e. palms and feet.
During the week of the operation against alias Alfonso Cano, Eliazar was warned to take clothes with him for at least three days.
Something very big was awaited.
On Friday at night he left for Cauca in an Palmira helicopter.
He knew that he had to identify two corpses and that it was suspected that one of them was that of the man who was running the oldest guerrrilla in the world, so he was asked to carry the card with Guillermo Leenz (his real name).
But when he arrived with the investigation team about the corpse, he didn't recognize it by its physical aspect, because it was very different from what he had seen in the media.
He didn't have a beard, he was clothed like a farmer in the area, without glasses and much leaner.
But fingerprints don't lying.
Accompanied by Galtonic magnifying glass, ink, collector and lantern, Eliazar compared feats of the corpse with those on the map.
His eye didn't deceive him: it was "Alfonso Cano."
On the same night, the man from Puerto Tejada was the official who confirmed to the Minister of Defence, Juan Carlos Pinzon, yes, he was the supreme leader of the FARC.
Searching for the impregn
According to a typist from Sijon de Cali the recognition that President Juan Manuel Santos made to Eliazar Gonzalez in a press conference for the operation against "Cano", is very noticeable.
A lot of people don't know our job.
Tactyloscopy is an extricated, undisturbed science, which can be crucial in the resolution of crimes.
Apart from locating carcasses, typists supported investigations into crimes such as theft and homicide and confirmed the identity of captured persons.
When they are captured, many delinquents have falsifying Identity Cards or do not have documents.
We take their fingerprints and look for them in the database.
The researcher admitted that in one flight he had to remove part of a wall from a house, because in this place the thief had pulled his hand to take out a safe full of jewellery and money.
'The walls are difficult because they do not have adequate areas to make a fingerprint, but in this case we were able to obtain the imprint with all the hand and identify the offender who had participated in different flights,' he recalls.
Footprints may also dismiss or confirm suspects in hustle and thievances.
Recently we found a dead man with drink bottles around him.
An interlude of the investigation it has been determined that a parent can be a suspect of the crime and indeed a fingerprint on the bottle coincided.
Even if it doesn't guarantee that he is the assassin, it puts him at the crime scene.
A CTI typist explained that "final fingerprints of objects with chemical or physical reagents" are extracted.
When they appear, a collector or a strip is used to take them.
Subsequently, the fingerprints are sent to Asif, the decay database, or to the National Civil Registry.
'The problem is that there are still many undocumented Colombians,' the CTI expert said.
He explained that not everything is how the CSI television programme is shown.
As anything that shines is not of gold, everything that one touches necessarily becomes an imprint.
It can, however, be used if the necessary confrontation is realized.
'Indignants defuse authorities in New York
Hundreds of demonstrators jostled in front of zuccotti Square for encampment.
A large proportion of the persons took the evacuation order and moved to a nearby park.
Occupy Wall Street members defied the New York authorities on Tuesday by trying to re-entry, thanks to a judicial order, instead of Manhattan, where they were emptied at dawn and when they tried to occupy another park in the Big Apple.
Hundreds of demonstrators jostled in front of zuccotti Square, where they wanted to gain access through a judicial order, which would allow 'indignants' to encamp again in this area, while the other part of the movement responded by occupying a park further north of the city, where there were new stops.
'Our idea is that if the court doesn't allow us to camp again in the square, we're going to move to another place and we'll continue the legal struggle to go back to zuccotti, through sit-ins and peaceful protests," said one of the spokespersons of the "Occupy Wall Street" movement.
Bray detailed that the response of the 'indignant' to the evacuation of their camp personally dictated by New York City Mayor Michael Bloomberg reached the courts, where Judge Lucy Billings issued an order which prohibited the forced evacuation of the place and allowed the protesters to remain in the camp.
In addition, the indemnities occupied a new park located at the confluence of the Canal Street Power Centers and Sixth Avenue, very close to the entrance to the Holland Tunnel, which unites New York and New Jersey, owned by Trinity Church parish.
At least for an hour they managed to occupy the place, but after the police moved there and arrested some demonstrators, even though Bray was unable to confirm the concrete figure, assuring Efe of the fact that he saw a few journalists handcuffed.
The organization assures that there were centenarians of people who had settled there, where a delegation of religious leaders moved in support to the members of the movement and tried to intervene on behalf of them before the police.
In the meantime, zuccotti - which is an enclosure of private property - remains closed pending the celebration of a judicial hearing scheduled for this Tuesday, during which we must analyse the legality of the evacuation this morning.
A few demonstrators who had been displaced until then worn and distributed at that time copies of this judicial order and even handed it over to agents who were monitoring the square, which caused 'some small clashes', Bray said, who denounced the fact that some officials beat the protesters.
The judicial order prohibits the authorities from evacuating members of Occupy Wall Street or applying 'laws published after the beginning of the occupancy or from preventing protesters from regaining access to the park with tents or other properties they used before'.
May Mayor Bloomberg told the press that, as a result of this judicial order, the city had suspended the reopening of the space to the public and to the protesters, to whom, however, he alerted that local laws did not allow them to settle with tents and sacking bags.
The municipal councillor personally defended the evacuation carried out at dawn in zuccotti Square, an operation during which 200 people were arrested and which was decided due to the fact that the protesters were 'raping the law'.
Bloomberg explained that he had given an order evacuating space because it became "a place in which people did not come to protest, but to violate the laws and in some cases to hurt others", because "some shops had received threats" and the residents were afraid "for their quality of life".
'This morning's evacuation has been repuglitated and shows that Bloomberg is more interested in defending the financial interests than those of American workers,' added the spokeswoman, accusing the mayor of 'not respecting freedom of expression'.
"Occupy Wall Street" officials have also indicated that they do not intend to organise "great actions" for Thursday in collaboration with community organisations and trade unions to commemorate the two months of protests, which began on 17 September.
Pamela Anderson will be the Virgin Mary in a special Christmas television program
Behind Michael Bublé and other celebrity Canadians, she will be featured in the television program "It's a Russell Peters Christmas" on December 1
Canadian Pamela Anderson was Tim Allen's assistant in "Home Improvement", a rehearser in "Baywatch", rabbit in "Playboy", wife of rock musician Tommy Lee and now the actress and model will be the Virgin Mary in a special Christmas program of Canadian television.
CTV Private Television Channel announced today that Anderson, next to "Crooner" Michael Bublé and other celebrity Canadians, will be released in the television program "It's a Russell Peters Christmas" on December 1, playing a very special Virgin Mary.
CTV today distributed images of the television program in which Anderson, also known for the widespread broadcast on the Internet of a private pornographic Video in which it appeared with her husband at that time, the drummer of "Motley Cree", Tommy Lee, appeared dressed like the Virgin Mary by supporting a doll that represented Jesus in a crèche.
In the scene, Peters, a famous Canadian comedian of Indian origin who recently presented part of Charlie Sheen "My Violent Torpedo of Truth," appeared as Joseph.
Next to Anderson and Peters, CTV's Noel program also features Canadian singer Bublé, who at the end of the March married in Buenos Aires the Argentinian Model Luisana Lopilato in Buenos Aires, which is not far from controversies.
Recently, Bublé called Kim Kardashian 'put' during one of his concerts in New York.
Japan resurrected after the Earthquake
GDP rises 1.5% thanks to domestic consumption and puts an end the downward spiral at the beginning of March
Since the month of March, the Japanese economy has been paralyzed, further convalescent after the devastating nuclear-circumshell that has become a nuclear-dispatch, the worst disaster in the nippon country since the Second World Wrave.
Preliminary estimates from the Tokyo Government have calculated the material losses at USD 300,000 million, a blow that, despite the difficult global context, appears to have eased.
After three consecutive trimesters of falls, Gross Domestic Product data between July and September were used to turn the page permanently: GDP increased 1.5% compared to the previous trimester and 6% in an annualised rate.
The second trimester of the second trimester – the fiscal year begins in March 2012 – also assumes the fastest increase from January to March 2010.
Seizure is partly due to the resumption of acquisitions of equipment to restore areas devastated by the Earthquake and post-tsunzashade.
In fact, companies increased their investments by 1.1%, compared to the 0.9% of capital expenditure observed from January to March, the first decline after six-month uninterruptible increase.
However, the true stimulation of the Japanese economy does not rely exclusively on the Keynesian pulchment to take advantage of the crisis to consolidate – or to rebuild – the infrastructions, to make use of the gaps to grow from scratch.
Japan has woken up mainly as a result of changes in domestic consumption, which currently accounts for 60 per six per cent of Japanese GDP.
This variable increased a 1%, driven by the increase in expenditure on vehicles, travel and entertainment activities.
Stigma of yen
Tokyo went back to the internal market when the yen – the Japanese currency – became one of the “shelter values” elected by investors fleeing the eurozone debt crisis in the eurozone and the turmoil of the United States, a slab that weighs like a slab in the accounts of the exporting companies.
With a stronger divide, products are more expensive abroad, a serious set-a set-a set-a setback for a country with an economic vehicular route determined by the health of major tech groups.
Toyota or Sony, for example, accounted for their low performance between July and September.
As markets continue their sieves in the out-of-ordinary EU countries, the Yoshihiko Noda government does not lower its guard.
'We need to keep abreast of the remaining risks, such as the deterioration of certain foreign economies, the impact of the floods in Thailand and the rapid appreciation of the yen," said Secretary of State for Economic Policy Motohisa Furukawa yesterday.
In fact, the prospects are not at all encouraging.
The Organization for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) mentioned that the majority of member countries presented clearer signs of an economic slowdown in September.
The anticipating inflection points in the global economy has highlighted the fact that the main powers are being held back.
It has fallen four decimal places up to 100.4 points, compared to 100.8 in August.
Euro-zone has even been below the average, which lies in level 100, with eight decimal places up to 99.13 points.
Athens represses doubts
Papadimos Says The Adjustments Will Approved, But the Conservatives won't sign any written commitments
With peripheral risk bounties unleashed and uncertainty resettled in the parks, Greece continues to entangle itself in the labyrinth that led to the emergence of the great European debt crisis.
The perception that the "consensus" of the new Hellenic Transitional Government is more imposed by the requirements of Brussels than by a real conviction of the political forces begins to be confirmed by the facts.
While the new Prime Minister Lukas Papadimos reported yesterday that there is no alternative to structural reforms and the 100-day period established in its designation may be short to implement them, the leader of the Conservative New Democracy Party, Antonis Samaras, was making sure that he was not going to support the new austerity measures in any case, and reiterated his demand for the planned new elections.
"The main task of this Government is to implement the decisions of the summit of 26 October", said Papadimos when it opened yesterday a parliamentary debate which will end tomorrow with a vote of confidence for the new Executive.
A package of 24 programmes to boost employment, the reform of the Civil Servants' Disciplinary Code and the new anti-avoidance measures will be the first initiatives.
However, the message of Samaras no raises doubts about its position: 'We are committed to assisting the Transitional Government, but we are not committed to anything more'.
The convegivative leader went a step further in its pulse than the EU and the IMF, the bodies that must unlock the EUR 8 000 million that Athens urgently needs, and has made it clear that it will not approve a letter of commitment to make the adjustments.
But the European Commission, irunderdriously irritated by Papandreu’s back-to-back referendum, reiterated yesterday that its demand is firm: it will not unleash aid if the new Government and the leaders of the main Hellenic parties don’t sign “a written commitment without ambiguities as soon as possible”.
The spokeswoman for Economic Affairs, Amadeu Altafaj, explained that troika inspectors would return "very quickly" back to Athens to discuss with the new government "the elements necessary to pay the sixth instalment" of 8 000 million, even if there is no final date.
The Greeks still have a lot to do.
Queen Sofia: 'These are instruments for progress and hope
The Queen points out the "pre-recording" place of Spain with regard to this type of finance
Spain "achieved a leading place at global level in the role of micro-finance".
Queen Sofia recalled that in the last two decades the country has become the second largest donor of the planet in the last two decades with regard to this instrument of co-cooperation whose 'the essence' lies in the fact that it is an 'an instrument for social progress, justice and hope in a better future for all of humanity, based on confidence in human beings'.
The Queen yesterday inaugurated the Fifth World Micro-credit Summit in Valladolid with the certainty that “there is no doubt” that this type of operation is “absolutely indispensable” in order to achieve the Millennium Development Goals established by the United Nations for 2015, and, “very especially, in those who aim to reduce to half the volume of people who live on incomes in one dollar a day, to which the “poverty is fixed”.
Spain "continues to work" to enlarge the network of beneficiaries "in all corners of the globe, and assumes this task with "responsibility", said Queen Sofia, who had hoped that the initiatives and contributions originating during the Summit, which would continue until Thursday "guarantee the creation of new capacities and better opportunities for the poorest and above all for women", the main beneficiaries of these loans.
We have 'achieved a lot of objectives' and 'we are facing a lot of challenges'.
The Microcredit Summit is seen not only as a forum for debate, but also as the platform from which "two basic goals must be promoted".
For example, Her Majesty explained that the goal is that at the end of 2015 there will be 175 million poor families who accessed these financial services and that 100 per hundred million of these families will emerge from the poverty line in which they now live.
The impetus in the conception of micro-credits in Spain and in the rest of the world 'followed the trajectory begun a few years ago by Muhammad Yunus, stressed Queen Sofia as an 'act of justice and due recognition.
And she described the father of this instrument as an “admirable idealist in the universal cause of the fight against poverty”, the work of which she has known since 15 years previously, when the Queen traveled to Bangladesh to see her lending policy on the ground.
This experience was followed a year later by the First World Summit in Washington, D.C. and other meetings such as those celebrated in Côte d'Ivoire in 1999, Canada in 2006, and "at each meeting I have seen a continuous change", explained His Majesty, whose relationship with this project gave him the adjective "familself" meeting in Valladolid on the part of the "Balking of the Poor".
Eventually, Queen Sofia ended its intervention by encouraging those present to participate in the debate and in the search for solutions to move forward in the fight against poverty.
"Impertive etiquette"
The Minister for Foreign Affairs, Trinidad Jiménez, has warned that solidarity 'is an ethical imperative' for any democratic state.
In doing so, she explained that it would be an error in combining cooperation with developing developing countries in times of economic prosperity, as this is a "permanent obligation".
Jiménez argued that micro-credit presupposes a "serious and rigorous policy dedicated to the eradication of poverty", and now 'more than ever' the governments of the developed countries must work under the criteria of solidarity and do not let it be the victim of the cuts.
For Spain, micro-credit is "one of the main lines of cooperation for development".
Secretary of State for International Co-cooperation, Soraya Rodriguez, applauded "succeed" in giving "confidence" to "exclusive people to whom the gate fits the gate"; the mayor of Valladolid, Francisco Javier Leon de la Riva, welcomed those present in a city that 'exerced its doors with a spirit of solidarity.
The CDU proposes to elect the European President by universal suffrage
The first day of the Christian Democratic-Democrancy (CDU (Christian Democratic Union (CDU), on Monday 14 November in Leipzig took place without real difficulty for Angela Merkel, chancellor but also president of the Christian Democratic Party.
The future of Europe was not originally foreseen in the programme but prevailed in the autumn, in particular to satisfy the activists who consider the government's policy too lax vis-à-vis the Greeks today and perhaps Italians tomorrow.
The Motion on the Presidency of the European Commission, proposed by management, was almost unanimously adopted.
Out of 1001 delegates, only nine voted against and ten abstained.
Angela Merkel and then, a few hours a few hours a few hours a few hours from the end of their speeches, the very Europhile Minister of Finance, the very Europhile Minister of Finance, gave no room for doubt.
Chancer's idea - and of the Motion - stems in a slogan: the response to the crisis 'is no less from Europe but more from Europe'.
Not only because Europe is peace, but because 'nine millions of jobs depend directly on the euro'.
For the chancellor, 'the time has come from a breakthrough in Europe'.
This could result in amendments to the Treaty of Lisbon to strengthen the European Union's control over the budgetary policies of the euro area countries, but it could also lead to the election of the President of the European Commission by universal suffrage "in order to give a face to Europe".
In any case, this is one of the proposals of the motion adopted by the Congress, whose infamous the infamous the infamous of Wolfgang Schauble.
