What does sick-have fraud represent?
After the allowances, the UMP and the government launched an offensive on sick leave.
Nicolas Sarkozy will be on Tuesday 15 November in Bordeaux to visit a family allowance fund, and to deliver a discourse on the theme - recurrent since 2007 - of the fight against social Fraud and the Assassination.
And to accompany this offensive, based on a desire to make economies in a context of rigour, but is no less heralding an axis of the right-wing presidential campaign, the majority wants to focus on sick leave.
First of all for the purpose of budgetary savings: the government is considering imposing on private employees on a fourth day of default before taking care of a stoppage of work.
Simply put, without a specific company agreement, an employee who falls ill would lose four days'wage, i.e. at less than 15 per cent of its monthly income.
A measure which risks arousing errration, but which would make it possible to bring back 280 million euros, according to Les Echos.
And which would be accompanied by another measure of more symbolic significance: to impose on civil servants a day of uncompensated absence in the event of a leave of work (they have so far had no waiting period, compared with three days in the private sector).
While these issues are under discussion, the majority are also re-enhaging the issue of Fraud, this time targeting abuses linked to work stoppages.
"Controls are going to be much more important and, in addition, if you are caught, you will repay," Xavier Bertrand told RTL on Sunday, November 13.
As in other cases, advertisements seem somewhat disproportionate to the reality of job arrest fraud.
Since 2002, it has been the subject of constant follow-up and political offensive, most of which have borne fruit.
The French are not sicker than elsewhere in Europe.
Let us dwell on the national figures first.
According to the National Health Insurance Fund (CNAM), more than EUR 237 million in daily allowances were issued in 2006.
According to the Research and Documentation in Health Economics Institute, daily allowances in 2008 accounted for 5 per five per cent of health-saving expenditure at EUR 11.3 billion.
Of this amount, 46% concerned maternity leave and accidents at work, and 54% concerned sick leave, i.e. EUR 6.2 billion.
And so 2.5 per cent of health-spending.
In 2010, the French experienced an average of 14.5 working days, compared to 17.8% in 2009, according to an Alma Consulting study.
An average that hid certain deviations: another survey, published in 2007 by the career management site Monster.com, showed that 75% of the 40,000 French employees surveyed said they did not take any sick-have days.
Another study, this time of sickness insurance, states that in 2010, 37% of sickness discontinuations lasted less than 8 days, 22% from 8 to 14 days, 15% from 15 days to one month, 15% from one to three months and 11 per cent beyond.
When compared with their European neighbors, the French wage earners are no more subsidised to work stoppages.
A long study carried out in 2010 by two CNRS researchers showed that between 1994 and 2001, the rate of overall absence (for health or non-health reasons) fluctuated between 10% and 11% in France, compared with 20% and 28% in the United Kingdom and 16% and 18% in the United Kingdom.
Sillience fraud accounts very little about undeclared work.
Investigative is only a small increase.
Reinforcing the legislation since 2002 means systematic checks on sick stoppages of more than 45 days.
In 2008, out of 1.5 million controls, CNAM noted that 13% of the 285,000 carried out for short-term stops were "unjustified or too long", i.e. 33,050 cases.
In the case of decisions of more than 45-daily, systematically controlled, there were 11 per cent "unsuitable or unjustified" cases of 1.2 million, i.e. 132,000.
Altogether a total of 169,000 “unjustified” cases, out of several million employees who had had a sick leave.
A figure which represents very little face, for example, that of undeclared work.
According to UMP MEP Dominique Tian, author of a report on the issue in June, it would amount EUR 9 to EUR 15 billion worth of tax losses per year, i.e. more than the total cost of the daily allowances, and therefore infinitely more than the fraud against them.
Papadémos: Greece will have to implement a new post adjustment plan
Greece will need a new adjustment programme to reshape its economy.
Prime Minister Lucas Papadémos told Parliament on Monday 14 November, adding that the hundred days given to his unity government would not be enough to accomplish this task.
'In order to continue efforts to restore the economy, we need the support of our European partners and a new fiscal adjustment programme", said the Head of the Government, who delivered his first public speech at the opening of the debate on the vote of confidence in Parliament on Wednesday.
The implementation of the decisions taken at the euro area summit on 27 October will be the "principal task" of the new government, since the country's participation in the euro area "is at stake", he added.
As a result, Mr Papademos predicted that the country's general government deficit would be reduced "around 9%" of GDP by the end of the year, from 10.6% in 2010 and 15.7% in 2009.
The right rejects new austerity measures
Antonis Samaras, the right-wing leader, on Monday supported the measures already approved in an attempt to emerge from the debt crisis, but warned that his party would not commit to further austerity.
"We will not vote in favour of new measures," he said at a meeting with the members of his party, New Democracy.
He expressed his agreement with the objectives of reducing the deficit and the debt reduction and the fight against squandering but hostile to any policy that would prevent economic recovery.
At Olli Rehn, the European Commissioner for Economic and Monetary Affairs, who warned that the IMF and the EU would not release the EUR 8 billion loan necessary without written guarantees from all parties that they would support the planned measures, Antonio Samaras replied that his speech was sufficient and that he would not sign anything under external pressure.
Euthanasia and Assisted Suspicia Must Legalize, says expert committee
Custodians Live in Denunciation of Declining Death According to the Royal Society of Canada
On palliative health care, the committee calls for governments, health-care institutions, and doctors to work together to ensure better palliative care beyond cancer.
Canadian society lives in the denial of death.
Only 9% of Canadians agreed to talk to their doctor about the conditions in which they wanted to die and make arrangements to that effect.
This is one of the conclusions of an impassion report by the Royal Society of Canada, in which participated six experts related to various fields, including ethics, law and medicine.
This committee recommends that the Government amend the Criminal Code to permit assisted and euthanasia assisted suicide, where appropriate, from a patient deemed competent to make such a decision.
From the outset, experts cited a recent survey by the Economist Intelligence Unit, which compared the quality of death in 40 countries around the world.
While Canada ranked tenth, the study noted that "the medicalization of death in Canada has created a culture in which people are afraid to address the subject of death."
Medicalisation or not, 77% of Canadians do not have access to palliative care, according to experts mandated by the Royal Society of Canada.
And despite the desire of most Canadians to die at home, 68.6 per cent eventually die in the hospital.
On the other hand, Quebec is the highest in Quebec, with 86 per six per cent of deaths occurring in hospital.
On the sensitive issue of euthanasia and assisted suicide, the authors of the report reached conclusions that run counter to the position of the Federal Government, which had already said that they did not want to change anything to the Criminal Code regarding euthanasia and assisted suicide.
However, according to the experts, which have also reviewed all the countries where these approaches are legalized or decriminalized, there is no evidence that de-criminalization leads to abuses in the application, i.e. the practice of euthanasia or assisted suicide on uncomplicated or unsuspecting persons.
On the other hand, Jocelyn Downie, co-signatory of the report, said yesterday, more cases of these overflows in countries where euthanasia and assisted suicide are not legalised.
Euthanasia is practised in Canada, where it is clearly illegal.
'Assisted suicide is practised in Canada, where it is clearly illegal,' explains Jocelyn Downie.
Authanasia Canadians
It should be recalled that 85 per cent of Canadians have already voted in favour of euthanasia because they believe that it would enable end-of-lives to alleviate their suffering, according to an Angus Reid survey conducted in 2010.
And 66% believe that legalizing euthanasia will not have the effect of sending the message that the lives of the elderly or people with disabilities are of less value.
With regard to persons engaged in assisted suicide, the respondents estimated at 41 per cent that they should not be prosecuted.
With regard to the maintenance or cessation of care for a patient, the situation is less clear, say the authors of the report.
Therefore, in the list of their recommendations, they suggest that it is stated in the Criminal Code that the withdrawal of care for which there has been a legally valid refusal is not criminal.
The expert committee also calls for healthcare professionals to be trained to respect an end-of-dwelling refusal, without fear of criminal prosecution.
On palliative health care, the committee calls for governments, health-care institutions, and doctors to work together to ensure better palliative care beyond cancer.
Finally, with regard to palliative or terminal seducation already widely used in Canadian health-care institutions, the committee calls for its consideration, when it does not serve to reduce physical pain as euthanasia, and be categorized with the same procedures.
Reservations from other experts
Some experts in the field of health law have already expressed reservations regarding the findings of the report of the experts of the Royal Society of Canada.
Pierre Deschamps, a specialist in health law, described as "extreme" the position of the experts signatories of the report, among other things because it does not limit the practice of euthanasia to patients in the final stages.
'This opens the door to a situation in which everyone who would be tired of life and who would be over the age of 18 could ask to be helping to commit suicide,' says Mr Deschamps, who yesterday had consulted a summary of the report.
Me Deschamps also points out that the authors of the report seem to place personal autonomy above all the other values that participate in the social fabric of society.
Now, 'in society,' he said, there are yardsticks and constraints.
Health law expert Margaret Somerville also expressed "strong opposition" to the findings of the report, which she described as 'pro-euthanasia protest'.
Margaret Somerville, a member of the Royal Society of United States of Canada, in particular disputes the data in the report as to the absence of abuse in certain countries where euthanasia and assisted suicide are legalized, i.e. the Netherlands and Oregon.
The King of Jordan Says President Assad to Leave the Power
Damas denunciates "conspiracy"
Syria is increasingly isolated
Proponents of Bashar al-Assad demonstrated yesterday outside the premises of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs.
Diplomatic pressure intensified yesterday on Bashar al-Assad’s Syria, which, with the support of Russia, resisted and denounced a “conspiracy” against the country.
Two days after the suspension of Syria by the Arab League, the European Union stepped up its sanctions against Damas, while Syria’s neighbour Turkey and Jordan took a stand in favor of President Assad’s departure.
King Abdullah II of Jordan was yesterday the first Arab ruler to call on President Bashar al-Assad “leaving power” in Syria, where some 40 civilians and members of the regime’s forces were again killed.
"While Bashar was at his country's interest, he should resign, but it should also create the necessary conditions for a new phase of Syrian political life," the king of Jordan, a neighbour of Syria, said in an interview with the BBC.
For his part, the head of Turkish diplomacy Ahmet Davutoglu lamented that the Turkish mediation efforts made since the beginning of the year with the Syrian regime had failed.
'Those which are not at peace in the Middle East with their peoples and cannot satisfy them will leave", he said, alluding to Syria, a neighbouring country towards which Turkey is adopting an increasingly hard line.
The United States, for its part, welcomed "strengthevious the consensus against Assad and the actions of the Syrian regime", following the decisions taken by the Arab League and the European Union.
"The International Community, the United States, the EU, the Arab League, countries such as Turkey are adopting an increasingly harsh tone" in the face of repression in Syria, noted Mark Toner, a spokeswoman for the State Department.
During this time, at least 16 civilians and at least 19 members of regime forces perished in the Dar’a region of southern Syria, the Syrian Observatory for Human Rights (OSDH) announced.
Two other civilians perished "when shooting and shelling heavy machine-gun fire in Jobar" in the besieged city of Homs, according to this NGO.
At a press conference, Syrian Foreign Minister Walid Mouallem said that the country was "creeping towards the end of the crisis".
Indeed, it reacted strongly to the decision of the Arab League to suspend Damas from its work, which, in his view, is a "dangerous step".
“Syria will not blur,” he added, assuring that "conspiracy against Syria will fail".
Following its decision to suspend Syria, the Arab Arab League is now studying a “mechanism for the protection of civilians” and wishes to send 500 members of Arab human rights organizations, the media and military observers in the country.
French Foreign Foreign Minister Alain Juppé was also in favour of sending UN observers to help to protect civilians from the repression of the regime which, according to him, locked itself into "paranoia".
A further extraordinary meeting of the league is planned tomorrow in Rabat to take stock of the follow-up to the implementation of the crisis plan decided on 2 November, including the withdrawal of the armed forces from the drifting cities and the release of the arrested demonstrators.
Russia’s foreign-Foreign Minister, Sergey Lavrov, yesterday called the Arab League’s decision “incorrect,” while China urged Syria to implement the crisis exit plan, while refraining from supporting possible sanctions against Damascus.
Italy
Monti demands time for markets and Italian sacrifices
The future head of the Italian government, Mario Monti, has demanded some time for the markets to train his team and implement a programme, which will contain "sacrifices", in order to regain the credibility lost in the last few months of the Berlusconi government.
The former European Commissioner was in discussions with the political parties yesterday before meeting trade unions and bosses today.
It should put in place a relatively small cabinet composed of technocrats outside Parliament.
'Monti talked about an important programme with a lot of sacruples,' said Francesco Nucara, a member of one of the many small parliamentary groups involved in the talks, after meeting the President of the Council-designate.
The President of the Chamber of Deputies, Gianfranco Fini, said he expected Mario Monti by Friday to call for a vote of confidence in Parliament to ensure that the new government had sufficient support.
The consultations will be concluded by Mr. Monti presenting a shortlist, arguably of a dozen ministers, to the Head of State, Giorgio Napolitano.
Mr Monti commented on the nervousness of the stock and bond markets yesterday, asking them for time.
“Democracy, there are specific deadlines” for preparing a government and its agenda, he pointed out, saying 'Second that the markets will be patient and will understand'.
Its designation by the president was initially greeted by the markets, but the concern picked up, especially after an unexpected fall in industrial production in the euro area in September.
The Prime Minister-designate should set up a team consisting mainly of technocrats, although he stressed that he would like to include "policies".
The new government is expected to run until 2013, the next legislation, he said.
According to the President of the Chamber of Deputies, Gianfranco Fini, the new government will gain parliamentary confidence by Friday.
Mr. Monti undertook, once taking up his duties, to work as a matter of urgency and with the aim of Italy 'responsibly racing' in Europe.
A spokeswoman for European Commissioner Olli Rehn stressed that "even with a new government our diagnosis of the Italian economy does not change".
The EU, convinced that Rome will not reach its balanced budget objective in 2013 despite the austerity plans adopted in recent months, has called in particular for new austerity measures.
The 'patron of bosses' Italian Emma Marcegaglia, who will see it on Tuesday, also stressed the need to revive the economy, 'because a non-growth country cannot comply with the deficit parameters.
Mr Monti, renowned for its competence and independence as European Commissioner (1994-2004), represented an "period change" for Italy after 17 years of "Barlusconism", the "Professor" symbolising "the defiance of seriousness" and "another Italy", according to the columnists.
The great unknown will be the longevity of his team.
President Napolitano would like to avoid early elections, because Italy will have to invest EUR 200 billion of state bonds by April 2012.
Russian Scientist Assisted Iran’s Nuclear Program
A Russian scientist Vycheslav Danilenko has helped Iran develop a detonator usable with a nuclear weapon, an atomic risk NGO said on Monday.
The Institute for International Science and Security (ISIS) relies on the recent International Atomic Security Agency (IAEA) report and other United Nations office documents to identify this researcher.
Last week, the International Atomic Energy Agency (IAEA) expressed "strengthening concerns" about an "possible military dimension" of Iran's nuclear programme.
According to ISIS drawing heavily from IAEA documents, Mr. Danilenko, born in 1934, worked for three decades since the 1960s at a Soviet military nuclear power in Chelyabinsk (Ulural) for three decades.
In 1989 or 1991, he reportedly left the centre to set up an enterprise producing "nano diamonds" in Kiev.
The economic difficulties of his company reportedly led him to contact the Iranian Embassy in Ukraine in 1995.
He reportedly reportedly cooperated on the Iranian programme from 1996 to 2002, before his return to Russia.
In its most recent report, the IAEA 'strong indications that the development by Iran of a nuclear detonation system' has been aided by the work of a foreign expert, who not only was well acquainted with this technique, but who, as a Member State has informed the Agency, worked for most of its career on this technology in the military nuclear programme of its country of origin".
Anti-Muslim crimes and misdemeanours increased by 50 per cent
Crimes and crimes against Muslims increased by almost 50 per cent from 2009 to 2010, while at the same time, other racial and religious violence has declined slightly or slightly, according to FBI statistics released on Monday.
According to these figures, the total number of violence perpetrated against Muslims fell from 107 in 2009 to 160 in 2010, an increase of 44 per cent, compared with an increase of 13 per cent in violence against Catholics, a 4 per cent decrease in offences against Jews and an overall 14% increase in anti-religious offences.
The total number "hatility crimes" increased very slightly to 6,628 cases, including 47.3% motivated by race differences and 20 per cent by religious differences, according to the FBI.
"After a decline in 2009, it is disruptive that these crimes and misdemeanours will increase again," the human rights organisation Human Rights First said, 'the increase in anti-Muslim violence is particularly significant,' the organisation added in a statement.
“Human Rights First has long stressed that violence against Muslims, as well as all forms of hate crimes, must be seen as a serious violation of human rights,” added Paul Legendre.
'The U.S. government can and must do more to tackle these abuses," he adds, believing that this could involve an improvement in police reports on "hatcreatic crimes."
"Hate crime" in U.S. federal law is a legacy of the epoch of the struggle for civil rights.
This legislation had been adopted after Martin Luther King's death to punish violence related to race, skin colour, religion, origin and now gender.
Twitter modifies its platform and looks more like Facebook
The micro-blogging-Twhiard site introduced changes in the layout of its platform on Monday to allow its users to observe the activity of those they follow, which makes its service a little bit more like that of Facebook.
A new 'activity' tab and another 'name' make it possible to more legitimize, live, the activity of the other "Twittos", Internet users whose messages ("tweets") and those who follow you.
This makes it possible to know whether they follow messages, whether they are subscribing to new accounts, whether they have favorite messages, etc.
