Austria
inclusion of a "gold rule" budget in the constitution
Austria’s social-Democratic/Democratic coalition government decided on on Tuesday to include in the constitution a “gold rule” budget in order to reduce public deficits and thus avoid a possible deterioration by the rating agencies of Austria’s sovereign rating (triple A).
Social Democratic Chancellor Werner Faymann himself announced this decision at the end of a Council of Ministers, when the Austrian public debt is 74.6%, well above the maximum criterion set by the Maastricht Treaty, but clearly below the debts of countries such as Italy, Greece or Spain.
'While Austria's creditworthiness were lowered by only one notch from AAA to AA, we would have to pay three billion more interest every year,' argued Vice-Chancellor/Democracy-Michacean-Ministry of Foreign Affairs Michael Spindelegger.
Austria is thus responding to a request at the Brussels Summit of the euro area member countries on 26 October: the Heads of State and Government then called for the inclusion by the end of the end of 2012 of the reduction of public deficits and a return to the balance of public accounts in the constitution or a law of equal value.
The mechanism adopted by the Austrian Government is approaching the "German model", with Germany having been the first European country to adopt such "rules".
By 2017, the structural general government deficit will have to fall by 0.75% of the Gross Domestic Product (GDP) each year and, as from 2017, the federal state's structural government deficit must not exceed 0.35% of GDP each year, while the regional states and the municipalities will have to present balanced budgets.
The objective is to increase public debt by 2020/21 under the 60% criterion laid down in the Maastricht Treaty.
Strong yen
Noda threatens new foreign-exchange intervention
Japanese Prime Minister Yoshihiko Noda warned on Tuesday that the Japanese authorities would intervene in the foreign-exchange market again should the yen continue to rise.
"We will intervene as we did the last time if we see excessive volatility" in the yen exchange rate, Mr Noda told the Senate.
On Tuesday, the yen went back not far from the historical levels that prompted the Japanese authorities to intervene on 31 October.
On that day, Tokyo sold yen massively against dollars in order to lower the value of the nipple currency, which had just set a new record of vigour since 1945 against the greenback.
But the yen has regularly come back since then, doped with purchases by investors who regard it as a "reserve value" at an unsecure time of global economic slowdown and debt crisis in Europe.
The United Dollar fell back on Tuesday under 77 yen, and the euro fell below the symbolic threshold of 105 yen, in close proximity to the levels that triggered the last intervention.
Prior to the 2008–2009 financial crisis, the United Dollar was around 120 yen yen and more than 160 yen.
This spike in the Japanese motto harms the archipelago’s exports as it adds to the cost of Made in Japan products and reduces the value of income from abroad by Japanese firms, when converting it into a national currency.
"Kiswan has been struggling to rebuild itself" since the March 11 earthquake and tsunami that devastated the Tohoku (north-east region), stressed Mr. Noda, who feared that this rising yen could kill the mahorrenal recovery into the egg.
The world’s third-largest economy resumed growth in the third quarter – 1.5 per cent compared to the previous tributary – for the first time in one year, thanks to a bounty in exports and consumption of households faltobed by the disaster.
But the Prime Minister ruled that the high cost of the yen did not reflect “the economic fundamentals of Japan”.
Finance Minister Jun Azumi called the Bank of Japan (BoJ), which on Tuesday opened a two-day meeting of its Monetary Politics Council to take all possible steps to weaken the yen.
But Deputy Governor Hirohide Yamaguchi warned that the BoJ had already taken "appropriate measures", implying that no further monetary easing was expected on Wednesday.
At its last meeting at the end of October, the issuing institute increased its purchases of treasury bills by 5,000 billion yen (EUR 47 billion), raising the ceiling for the amounts it spends on the purchases of various financial securities to inundating the liquidity market.
Euro area:
Rates are rising, Stock markets falter
The risk of contagion of the debt crisis still undermines European indices.
Despite the appointment of new Heads of Government in Greece and Italy and better growth figures in France and Germany, rates from vulnerable countries continue to rise.
Always worried about the stability of the euro area, European Stock Exchanges shoot a little deeper into negative territory on Tuesday, 15 November.
In Paris, at 10 a.m., the CAC 40 lost 1.4% to 3,064 points.
The Dax in Fragment Frankfurt cedes 0.4% and London 0.1%.
"Along with the new tensions on the borrowing yields of troubled euro-zone countries, equity markets around the world continue to yield some of their recent gains."
Investigators are all too aware of the risk of conaggivation,” explains Terry Pratt, an institutional broker at IG Markets, quoted by Reuters.
The Tokyo Stock Exchange closed down by 0.7%, in turn suffering from renewed concerns of market participants about Europe's ability to contain the debt crisis.
"Kisno, like the rest of the world, has an eye on Europe and trade will continue in a narrow corridor of variation until investors have evidence that the situation has stabilized," said Mitsushige Akino, fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management.
This renewed anxiety about the eurozone’s future is penalising the single currency, which continues to decline on Tuesday.
The euro evolves at its lowest level in a month-and-a-half.
Around 10 a.m., the European currency is worth USD 1.3570 in decline by 0.4%.
According to brokers, it receded after Chancellor Angela Merkel, the CDU party, meeting in Congress on Monday, adopted a motion including the possibility for a country in difficulty to exit the eurozone without leaving the European Union without leaving the European Union.
Italian and Speas rates well above 6%
Borrowing rates in Italy and Spain remain at very worrying levels.
On Tuesday Morning, the yield on Italian ten-year bonds reached 6.8 per cent, the return on Spanish securities of the same maturity of 6.2 per cent.
These tensions over the interest rates of the countries of the South, but also in France, continue to fuel the fears of the interveners.
The head of the future Italian government, Mario Monti, demanded time from the markets to restore the situation in Italy, threatened with asphyxiation by its debt burden.
New Greek Prime Minister Lucas Papademos stated that the implementation of the decisions taken at the eurozone summit on 27 October would be the "principal task" of his government.
Spain ends up in the spotlight of the markets, while parliamentary elections in the country are being held on Sunday.
The “spread” spread between the 10-year bonds of Germany and Spain has reached a new all-of-the-shortener.
Waiting for growth figures in the euro area
On the statistical side, France recorded economic growth of 0.4% in the third trimester, slightly better than the  0.3 per cent awaited by household consumption.
However, INSEE revised the second-quarter figure on a downward decline of 0.1 per per cent of Gross Domestic Product (GDP) rather than stagnating.
And it's really the fourth that worries economists the most.
Germany, for its part, grew by 0.5% over the same period.
An initial estimate for the euro area as a whole is expected at 11 a.m. when the European Commission has warned of the risk of a new recession.
Energy policy:
facts must take precedence over dogmas
If it is a subject that cannot be dealt with on the basis of short-term or emotional considerations, this is energy policy.
Availability and the cost of electricity have a direct impact on the purchasing power and competitiveness of businesses.
It is healthy that an electoral event should provide an opportunity to reflect on such issues and the place where the nuclear industry is involved.
The debate will be fruitful only if it explores the overall economic, social and environmental consequences of the proposed options.
The French must base their opinion on objective facts and rational data.
With 7 billion human beings on the planet, demographic change will lead to a doubling of the demand for electricity by 2050, without depriving a huge part of the world's population of a vital element.
At the same time, it will be necessary to remedy the scarcity of fossil resources in order to continue to generate electricity on a permanent basis, without forgetting the urgent need to reduce greenhouse-gas emissions in order to combat climate-bludgetisation, a subject that has disappeared from the ecological debate.
The Fukushima accident does not change any of these parameters.
That is why Germany remains lobbied in its decision to abandon nuclear power.
This is not the case of Belgium, which exposes itself to the need to be able to find an alternative source capable of replacing either Switzerland, which just intends to build only state-of-the-art power stations.
In contrast, Great Britain, Finland, Poland, the Czech Republic, the Netherlands, Sweden, South Africa, South Africa, China, India and Brazil, to name but a few, are continuing their projects with determination.
The United States, for its part, has decided after Fukushima to add a unit to its park by resuming the construction of a power plant interrupted after the Three Mile Island accident.
While the German model, focused on renewable energy, is sometimes presented as an example, the reality frustrates the discourse.
The choice of Berlin will result in an increase in the price of electricity, an energy dependency aggravated by the increased use of imported gas, in particular from Russia, a leap in CO2 emissions caused by the construction of gas and coal-fired power plants.
Within a few months, all these unpleasant effects are already being felt.
The case of Denmark has to meditate.
Europe's Wind Champion, from which it generates 30% of its electricity, it is also one of the largest users of coal and gas due to the intermittence of the wind; by virtue of which its CO2 emissions and the price of its electricity are respectively 65% and 50% higher than the European average.
On the other hand, thanks to the constancy of our energy policy, our neighbours pay an electricity bill 40% higher than that of French households.
Whereas in a globalised economy the cost of energy is a key factor of competitiveness and a guarantee of maintaining the industrial tool in the national territory, French companies benefit from the cheapest electricity in Europe.
As a result, any significant reduction on the part of nuclear power would lead to a sharp increase in the price of electricity, which would make the good declarations on the reindustrialisation of our country and the defence of purchasing power.
A price which a large majority of French people refuse to pay, especially since they show no opposition in principle to this energy, the opinion polls show this.
It should be recalled that our country's energy policy has made it possible to build an industrial chain of 125,000 direct jobs and 410 000 jobs.
Highly export-oriented, it drives a gal galaxy specialising in international markets and makes them very creeivable of non-delocalizable jobs.
If France were to decide to step out of nuclear power, it would be to sacrifice many of these SMEs by reducing the annual EUR 6 billion of French exports of nuclear equipment and services.
Which electrician would order an EPR designed to operate for sixty years while the decline of its manufacturer would be programmed?
In order to solve the energy equation, it has to be recognized that there is no source of miraculous electricity generation, nor any diabolical.
Areva’s development of low-emitting CO2, non-availability solutions is an indication of the complementarity of energy sources.
For nuclear power, maintaining the highest level of safety is an absolute imperative.
In France, we have a safety authority which exercises an intransigent and transparent control over the existing power stations as on the Flamanville yard.
Our well-recognized teams, whose professionalism is inhabited by the same obsessibility.
In half a century, the nuclear industry has experienced three serious accidents.
Only one, Three Mile Island, intervened under conditions of normal operation; it remained without human or environmental consequences.
At Chernobyl, the design of the unforgivable jeoparry and human flaws caused the drama.
In Fukushima, some seem to be forgetting that the accident is the result of two unprecegenved natural disasters on an unprecedented scale that caused the death of tens of thousands of people.
The nuclear industry will draw lessons from this accident, as it did for the previous two.
The EPR, which is the result of this approach and of cooperation between the French and German security authorities, the EPR presents a concept making it capable of withstanding such phenomena.
While it was considered by all electricians wishing to build a power station, the end of its construction in Flamanville would be a magnificent gift to the competitors of Areva and EDF by leaving them free to seize leadership in the inescapable nuclear development.
It is indispensable and ledelagration to seek the best energy mix for our country, but it would be irresponsible to allow emotion, dogmas and partisan maneuvers to dominate such a decisive debate for the economic, social and financial situation in our country.
Is immigration a burden or an opportunity for the economy?
The immigration policy carried out under Nicolas Sarkozy has been criticised from a variety of angles - the recruitment of the police, judicial and administrative services subject to the policy, compatibility of this policy with the self-proclaimed 'human rights country' of France.
More recently, it is the administrative harassment to which foreign students are subjected which has made the news, the Minister of the Interior (liberation of 23 May) proclaiming in the way that France "does not need foreign talents, masons and restaurant waiters".
But it is only rarely analysed from an economic point of view.
It is because there is a fairly broad consensus on this subject between left and right.
On the right, the tone was set by Jacques Chirac who stated in 1976 that '9090000 unemployed should not be a problem in a country comprising nearly 2 million migrant workers', and on the left, by Michel Rocard explaining that France "cannot accommodate all the misery of the world" in 1990.
The only difference, the degree of generosity
In 2005, the fear of the invasion of the national territory by hordes of Polish plumbers was shared on both left and right.
Immigrants on both sides are seen as a burden for the French economy and society.
The only difference lies in the degree of generosity to immigrants.
In its programme, François Hollande confines himself to trivialities, indicating that co-development will make it possible to solve our "migration problem".
This idea of immigration as a burden, as a problem, may be an electoral payment; but it is economically very costly.
As economists Ian Goldin and Geoffrey Cameron point out in a recent book synthesizing the most up-to-date knowledge of immigration (Exceptional People: How Migration Shaped Our World and Will Define Our Future, May 2011, Princeton University Press, 352 pp.), there is a broad consensus among specialists on the positive impact of migratory flows on economic growth, wages and jobs in countries that receive immigrants.
Restricting immigration aneminous growth and hindering employment.
Fears about the impact of migrants are based on the assumption that they may replace national workers, especially low-skilled workers, exerting downward pressure on their wages.
However, experience shows that, in fact, immigrants are much more complementary than alternative to national wage earners, working mostly in sectors with a shortage of jobs.
Low-skilled immigrants work in sectors that do not attract national employees, and the most highly skilled in dynamic sectors in which training does not follow the supply of jobs.
A positive effect on wages
In the same way that a surgeon will find it very difficult to work in a country with a shortage of anaesthetists, these complementarities between nationals and migrants mean that the arrival of immigrants have a positive effect on the wages and employment of nationals.
Giovanni Peri calculated that an increase of 1 per cent in migratory flows leads to an increase of between 0.6 and 0.9 per cent in long-term real wages.
Without taking into account the fact that the diversity provided by immigrants contributes to the creation of ideas and economic growth (strong proportion of naturalized immigrants among American Nobel laureates; Google, Intel, Paypal, eBay and Yahoo were founded by immigrants).
Migrants are also net contributors to the social systems, at an average rate of 1 per cent of the total budget in European countries.
The World Labour Organisation, for example, estimates that in Germany an immigrant arriving at the age of 30 will contribute EUR 150000 to public budgets on an average of EUR 150000.
Nicholas Sarkozy, at the beginning of his term of office, wanted to look for the missing growth points 'with their teeth'.
The tooths in question, while deterring immigrants, had the exact opposite effect.
Europe has invented pro-democracy,
Democracy is a precious asset; it is also a fragile asset.
The European mainland should be able to remember it which, for having invented it in Athens more than two thousand years ago, experienced throughout the 20th century the tragedies of the Great War, totalitarianism, the holocaust, the gulag, but also Franco in Spain, Salazar in Portugal, the colonels in Greece.
Some may have believed that it would be sufficient for democracy definitively to mound its enemies to exercise the vote and to exercise it in the most direct manner.
Since Napoleon we have known, since Napoleon 'the Little', that this was an ease of thought.
Universal suffrage, in order to be the necessory condition of democracy, is not the sufficient condition and can even become an “instrument of oppression,” as the republican philosopher Etienne Vacherot once said.
No, democracy requires much more: a public space, a public spirit, values, an organisation, a separation and a control of powers, an education, an enlightenment, an economic and social solidarity, a judiciary.
The crisis in Europe we are experiencing is an economic crisis, but it is just as much a crisis of politics and pro-democracy.
In this respect, the palindy of the true Greek referendum will have served as a two-fold revealer: that of the need for a powerful return to politics, but also of the disturbing feeling of its impossibility, its incongruity.
This ambivalence must be designed in order to be overtaken.
The truth is that the front lines of the democratic struggle have now been displaced, and for many of them republican soldiers no longer shoot in the right direction.
We have nothing to gain by demanding a succession of national referendaggencies, organized according to necessity and in a fragmented manner.
A bleach to the European absolute monarch or a leap in the emptiness of the nationalist retreat: what choice for the citizen.
No, we don’t want chords, we do not want chord-cuts but a common, straight, serene and continuous exercise of choice of choice and of democracy, this time at European level.
There are no crises in Greece, Italy, Spain and Portugal - there can no longer be more to stop listing - because there is only one crisis, and it is in Europe.
It is Europe as a whole that complies with the dictates of markets and credit rating agencies, which suffers from impoverishment and its lack of solidarity.
It is Europe as a whole that is humiliated on the international stage.
We are worried about the transfers of sovereignty to Europe - but it is the whole of Europe that slowly loses its sovereignty, and with it each of its nations.
Urgency lies in restoring popular sovereignty the management of the European project.
It is together that Europeans can reduce the burden of their debts, free themselves from the markets and prepare for the future by investing.
Breathing out of the crisis will involve decisions at European level that cannot be taken without total democratic legitimacy, except, of course, leading us to disaster.
After a long period of combating this proposal by the socialists and Jacques Delors, everyone today is calling for the European Economic Government.
Let's say.
But blrow without stronger political and democratic integration, experienced as such by the citizens, would be new madness and a new impasse.
We must assume a new stage in the construction of Europe.
To make the republic in France, it was necessary to "make" Republicans.
The revolution in interests could not be enough.
This lesson remains valid.
We need to build a more economically integrated Europe.
This can only be done by constructing a more politically democratic Europe, genuine European popular sovereignty.
To make a difference in Europe, let us make Europeans.
“Contactless” smart cards are generalised
The World Smart Smart Show opens on Tuesday in Villepinte, near Paris, in an optimistic atmosphere.
The year of 2011 is expected to complete an 11 per cent increase in global sales of microprocessor cards (bank card, telephone with SIM card, etc.), to more than 6 billion units, according to Eurosmart.
The busiest segment is contact-free technology that makes it possible to pay for public transport or to pay for purchases by simply approaching the card of a reader.
This year, 460 Million Contactless Smart Cards should be sold around the world, compared to 320 million a year earlier.
Their share is expected to increase again in 2012 (580 million units expected) and to account for almost 10% of the global smart card market (6.925 million cards).
Lagged behind the Asia-Ph Asia and the Asia-Phase, Europe is gaining ground for non-contact payment.
Some EUR 26 million of Visa bank cards are already equipped with this functionality and 75,000 payment terminals accept it.
In France, demarches are more modest: 400,000 Visa contactless cards have been deployed.
But the large surfaces put the double bites.
Carrefour issued 2.5 million contactless MasterCard Pass cards this year.
Moving at a higher speed, it is now necessary for banks to equip their clients.
'Two major French companies have committed themselves to systematically distributing bank cards to their customers in the future, says Gérard Nébouy, Managing Director of Visa Europe France.
Banks are testing mobile payment
But this is only a first step towards the means of payment of the future: the mobile phone.
Few devices are currently equipped with NFC (Near Field Communication) technology, which is essential for conducting transactions.
'They are lagging behind.'
'But they should finally have a real rise next year.'
'We are counting on the sale of between 80 and 120 million units in 2012 worldwide,' explains Marc Bertin, president of Eurosmart.
But the banks take the lead.
Crédit Agricous announced yesterday the launch of a test from December to June in Caen.
The iPhone of some 200 customers and collaborators will be equipped with a case that allows contactless payment.
The BPCE has a similar project in Strasbourg and Marseille.
And Societe General, too, said yesterday to prepare an offer on the market that would allow its customers to pay without contact with their mobile.
From Saharans to Nigeria, the terrorist front expands
Paris was concerned about the links between al-Qa'idah in the Islamic Maghreb and the Boko Haram sect.
Suspicion of a long-standing suspicion, the links between al-Qa’id al-Qa’iamond in the Islamic Maghreb (Aqmi) and the Nigerian cult Boko Haram are confirmed.
On Sunday, Algerian Deputy Foreign Minister Abdelkader Messahel even stated that Algiers has now 'gissed its certainty'.
'The way in which the two organisations operate and the intelligence reports show that there is good cooperation,' he explained without further details.
Paris seems to make a similar analysis.
The investigation into the kidnapping of two French people in the Niger last January reportedly uncovered the gateways.
Kidnapped in a restaurant in Niamey from Aqmi, Antoine de Léocour and Vincent Delory were to be killed the next day at the same time as several of their kidnappers during an operation launched by the French special forces.
In the rubble of the intervention, French and Nigerian investigators had to find several telephone flesheries belonging to the terrorists.
Terrorist shaft
Animation of the appeals made is directed towards several interlocutors based in Mali, Niger and Nigeria.
According to Radio France Internationale (RFI), two issues are of particular interest to the investigators: one leads to a Nigérien who stayed extensively in Maiduguri, a town in the north of Nigeria, the cradle of Boko Haram and the other to a man who claims to be close to Aqmi and the cult.
'This is a step forward, but more formal evidence may need to awaited before talking about operational co-cooperation between Aqmi and Boko Haram.
"But it's possible."
'It has been known for years that the cult receives external financial and intellectual support and is becoming increasingly active,' tempers Kunle Amuwo, a Nigerian researcher.
In recent months these "pressors" had become more and more evident in Boko Haram's developments and discourses or its modus operandi, which now involve the use of suicide bombers or increasingly complex explosive devices, with the same bill as those used by Aqmi.
The international community, starting with Washington, D.C. has therefore put pressure on Abuja to take into account the challenges posed by Boko Haram.
For a long time, the Nigerian authorities had regarded the group as an enlightened unhappily community of interest.
Quick success
Founded in 2002 in Maiduguri around a Mosque, a school and the fundamentalist iman Mohammed Yusuf, Boko Haram - which means "Weather is a sin" - advocates the establishment of an Islamic emirate in north-Muslim.
In this poor region, the association is a rapid success.
It politicized itself and demanded strict respect for the sharia, while its militants engaged in the fight against the impious central government.
The latter are aimed primarily at churches, bars and administrations.
In 2009, Boko Haram activists were particularly active, increasing attacks on police stations.
The reaction of Abuja is, as usual, unsophisticated.
The army invaded the city, there were 800 deaths.
Mohammed Yusuf, arrested alive, is killed in prison.
As a result, the cult became radicalized and left its northern fiefdoms.
At last Christmas, she claims an attack that killed 80 people in Jos, followed by another in Abuja.
In June, Boko Haram attacked the safe police HQ during a suicide attack, his first.
In August, the local headquarters of the United Nations will be its first international target.
The creation of a terrorist shaft in Africa that sails from Mauritania to Somalia through Nigeria is now a matter of concern to all security experts.
While Aqmi invaded Mali and the Niger, as the al-Shabaab Somalis operate in Kenya, Boko Haram appears to be a new threat at the heart of the mainland.
Over the past week at an interview with President Goodluck Jonathan in Nigeria, Alain Juppé warned against Boko Haram and said he was willing to "share all the information".
