The Nord Stream, a new burgeoning of Russian gas in Europe
The Nord Stream gas Pipeline, directly linking Western Europe to Russia at the bottom of the Baltic Sea, came into operation.
It must supply gas for the equivalent of 30 million households.
Gazprom, the Russian gas giant, wanted to celebrate the event with brilliance.
More than 500 guests, including four Heads of State and Head of Government, last Tuesday, took part on the shores of the Baltic, between an orchestra and buffet, in the inauguration of the Nord Stream, this new gas pipeline that directly connects Russia with Germany under the sea.
Symbolically, German Chancellor Angela Merkel, Russian President Dmitry Medvedev, French Prime Minister François Fillon, and his Dutch counterpart Mark Rutte jointly turned the steering wheel of the Valve controlling the entry of Russian gas into the Western-European network.
With this new gas Piece, 27.5 billion cubic metres of additional Russian gas will arrive each year in Western Europe via a first 1,224-kilometre tube connecting the Saint-St. Petersburg region of Mecklymburg-Vomerania.
A second tube must be completed at the end of 2012, allowing a total of 55 billion cubic metres of gas, the equivalent of all French consumption.
And we are already talking about the possibility of constructing a third one on the same path.
For Gazprom, the timely completion of this huge garbage construction is victorious.
The project had been strongly contested by Poland and the Baltic countries.
The Nord Stream will make it possible to deliver gas directly to Germany, avoiding any overland passage by a third country (Poland or Ukraine).
As a result, these two countries are losing some of their power: it makes it more difficult for them to weigh on Russia by threatening to close access to Western European markets.
At the height of the debate, Warsaw had not hesitated to compare the Nord Stream to a "New German-Soviet Pact", referring to the Alliance between Hitler and Stalin to occupy Poland in 1939.
This means whether the subject is politically sensitive.
Western Europe, by providing support for this Russian project, has gained greater security of its supplies.
But this is at the cost of a weakening energy Europe, since the project created a gap between Eastern and Western Europeans.
In just another five-year-old, Russia has witnessed its vision of a direct alliance between Russia and the wealthy Western countries, which are heavily consuming gases, whose deposits are in decline.
"This pipeline is a component of our partnership with Russia, it is a new artery that binds us organically", François Fillon confirmed by inaugurating this gas pipeline, demonstrating how both the major gas contracts are political as they are.
All of this explains the broad smiles displayed by Gazprom’s leaders, present in large numbers in Lubmin, at the point of arrival of the gas.
The construction of this gas pipeline is also a technical feat.
The submarine tube is made of concrete-wound steel in order to protect it from the anchors of ships.
It rests on the bottom of the sea, at an average of 200 m.
In order to build it, it was necessary to avoid mine fields dating from the Second World War.
'A hundred had to be kidnapped or destroyed as a precaution,' said Jens Muller, spokesperson for the company Nord Stream.
There has also been an increase in measures to protect fauna and flora.
The tube is a long snake of metal, heavy like 242 Eiffel towers, that nothing interrupts its entire length: neither valve nor a maintenance station.
A monitoring platform was planned off the coast of Sweden, but project officials gave it up when the Swedes were unwilling to see a Russian state-owned company establish itself permanently off their shores.
"In the event of damage, we stop the passage of the gas in less than a minute", says Jens Muller.
In the event of a leak, the gas contained in the pipeline therefore escapes into the air.
But this case is considered unlikely by manufacturers who have planned welds for leak every 100 000 years.
The state of the gas pipeline is monitored by an underwater robot, dragged by a ship, for external observation, as well as an aircraft operating inside the gas pipeline.
In the shape of a cigar, it is introduced inside the tube, one meter innumerable innumerable, and it is pushed by the gas.
Upon arrival, after three full days of travel at a speed of 3 meters second, it provides precise information on possible deformities of the metal.
The gas Pipeline was designed to operate for 50 years without having to undergo any retrieval.
Circulation of the gas is simply ensured by the pressure.
It peaked at the start of 200 bars, and 100 on arrival.
Five companies joined forces to carry out this project.
The lender remains Gazprom, which retains 51% of the shares.
German E.ON and BASF (15.5% each) have been joined, as well as Dutch Gasunie and the French group GDF Suez (9% each).
These five companies have set up a joint venture called Nord Stream, which is responsible for the construction and operation of the gas Pipeline.
Its registered office in zoug, Switzerland, where taxation is particularly favourable.
Gazprom then leased the entire gas transmission capacity to Gazprom for a 20-year revolving company.
"Gazprom will pay for the total capacity, no matter how much gas actually transported," says Jens Muller.
Funding for the construction of the gas Pipeline was secured at 30 per 30 per 100 per cent and 70 per cent through bank loans.
The total cost of the work is expected to reach EUR 7.4 billion for both tubes.
For GDF Suez, e.g. investment amounted to USD 240 million.
But minority shareholders are sure to go into cost, since only Gazprom assumes the risk.
The gas pipeline emerges from the Baltic to Lubmin on a sandy walnose shore.
Valves, 4.5 meters high, form the gas outlet gate.
Sensors measure the pressure, the temperature and the specificities of the gas arriving before it is transferred to German gas pipelines.
Since last Tuesday, this terminal has received one million cubic meters of Russian gas every hour.
At full capacity, it will be EUR 3 million per hour and in 2012, EUR 6 million.
Moleskine, a branded notebook.
Created some fifteen years ago by an Italian company, the Moleskine wants to restore the taste of writing.
It has a perfume of adventures, hours spent writing in the light of a candle.
The Moleskin notebook is easily recognizable, with its black rectangular cover, eldastic, sheets of an off-white color, like aged by time.
However, the Moleskine notebook as we know it today was born... in 1997.
The Milan-based Italian company Modo Modo decided at the time to launch a design but distinguished notebook.
Maria Sebregondi, then consultant for the company, is in charge of the launch of the new product.
The notebook is inspired by an old production technique, the mole skin (myle skin) which denotes varnished wool used to cover benches, for example.
This type of notebook was very popular with writers and travelers.
" Moleskine revived an old and forgotten object, which was an icon of avant-garde performers until the 20th century", says Maria Sebregondi, having since become the head of the brand at Modo Modo.
The success is there.
Between 2006 and 2010, sales increased by 26 per cent to 12.7 million coins.
We are a long way from confidential production in the early years, when only 3,000 notebooks were designed.
Moleskine goes beyond the Italian borders and is exported to 70 countries from France to the United States.
It's difficult to draw up a robot portrait of the buyer, because the notebook seduces both the businessman, the student and the professionals.
Like Moleskine, its consumers are cosmopolitan, open-minded and cultured.
The small Italian company, which numbered 12 in its infancy, now has nearly 100 today and has had two offices since 2008, one in Milan and one in New York.
It distributes its notebooks in bookstores, from Fnac to small down-city shops and has established its identity.
'We've followed the course of time and this new need for writing, despite the rise of e-mails,' explains Maria Sebregondi.
This cultural signature accompanies Moleskine development: recently, the brand has recently launched an "Detour" exhibition where architects or artists exhibit their own notebook.
In August, it also launched a leopard blanket at the Locarno Film Festival.
Taking advantage of its success and this cultural aura, Moleskine has managed to diversify its products: sacks, diary, pens, computer covers ... The brand is available on a whole range of supports.
An envious success: in the autumn of 2006, Modo Modo was bought by the investment fund SG Capital Europe, now Syntegra Capital, writing a new page in Moleskine's history.
Why does the unemployment rate of workers with disabilities remain so high?
Around 19% of people with disabilities are looking for a job.
The 15th Week for the Employment of the Handicapped Open Skies begins today.
Despite a pro-voluntear public policy put in place in 2005, the unemployment rate for people with disabilities is still twice as high as 19%, compared with 9 per cent for the population as a whole.
Disadvantaging?
"Non," said Éric Blanchet, Director-General of Adapt, the association that is organising the Week for the Employment of Disabled Persons.
"The progress made over the last fifteen years is considerable."
"At the time, the unemployment rate wasn't two but three times higher."
'But there is still a great deal to be done.'
It had to be said that for unemployed persons with disabilities, there remained a large number of obstacles before gaining access to employment.
They are rather old: 37% are more than 50 years of age (17% on average).
They have a low level of qualification: 80 per cent do not have baccalaureate and of the 2.3 million students in higher education, only 10 000 are disabled.
Finally, long-term unemployment is very widespread: 53% for the disabled, compared with 38% on average.
In a nutshell, there are also criteria which particularly complicate the return to work and which "paste the crux of the problem", according to Christian Grapin, the general manager of Tremplin, an association set up in 1992 to help companies to recruit workers with disabilities.
So much for objective brakes.
There remains the untold mentalities that evolve, of course, but too slowly.
A study carried out in April by the association IMS-Entreprendre for the Cité indicated that the managers interviewed had "a low level of knowledge of the handicap", with an "over-ex-representation of people in wheelchairs, psychic or intellectual deficient".
As a result, private enterprises with more than 20 employees are still far from reaching the employment target of 6 per cent of disabled workers, as laid down in an Act of 1987.
The latest known figures, dating from 2008, show a ratio of 2.6 per cent.
'We are now around 3%,' says Pierre Blanc, Director-General of Agefiph, the body responsible for collecting contributions from companies that do not respect this law.
Proof: the number of "taxed" establishments, as did the amounts paid, decreased between 2007 and 2011, from 59,000 to 46 000 (from EUR 606 to EUR 480 million).
"The law of 2005, which increased contributions, created a lot of pressure on businesses, explains Pierre Blanc.
"But this improvement also reflects real awareness, especially in large groups."
According to the Ministry of Employment, only 59 per cent of establishments between 20 and 49 employees employ workers with disabilities, compared with 82% of enterprises with 100 to 199 employees.
